      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>G ROVIR YOUR C DOUGALL'S PATIINT SELF-RAISING FLOUR WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>G ROVIR YOUR C DOUGALL'S PATIINT SELF-RAISING FLOUR WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>London Society long ago discovered that one of the pleasantest places in warm weather was the terrace of the Houses of Parliament, and on most days it is crowded with tea parties • Strange to say the Ministerial Whips regard them from the point of view of the more the merrier, as though members are never reluctant to sacrifice their legislative duties in order to entertain ladies to strawlserries and ice creams, on the River front yet they are kept within call, and are ready to help in the division lobby. It has been suggested that the Government might provide a band for the terrace out of the £30,000 it is proposed to expend upon extending the dining and tea rooms at the Palace of Westminster. Included in these plans is a tea room behind the Ladies Gallery in the House of Commons. The trial of Earl Russell on a charge of bigamy has revived an old constitutional procedure, which but very few now living could ever have seen in practice. There have been only four trials in the House of Lords since the reign of George H, that of the Duchess of Kingston in 1776, also being for the offence of bigamy. The last trial was that of Lord Cardigan in 1841, for duelling, so that Lord Russall can justly claim to have provided bis country with what is quite a novelty in these modern times. . .</p>
        <p>Scores of workmen have been busy making temporary arrangements for bolding the trial, which one hardly knows whether to regard seriously or not. To most people it will no doubt seem a ridiculous fuss to make over the matrimonial affairs of a man who would scarcely be known to the public had be never married at all.</p>
        <p>IThere are only very few people remaining who were present at the Coronation of Queen Victoria, but it is quite within the probabilities that some who attended that event will also be at the Coronation of Edward VII. The Earl of Leicester, who was a page to the Duke ot Sussex, at Queen Victoria's Coronation,is not yet eighty and still in vigorous old age. The same may be said of the Baroness Burdett Coutts, who was present on that occasion, and who, in spite of her eighty-seven years, is, still able to get about. The Duke of Cambridge and his sister the Grand Duchess of Mecklenburg,-Strelitz, were also present at the last Coronation and finally must be mentioned the aged Lord Gwydyn who was born in 1810, and has witnessed no fewer than three Corona- tions, namely those of George IV, William IV, and Queen Victoria. Princess Louise, Duchess of Argyll, has never cared much for Inverary Castle, her Argyllshire home, and this is perhaps one reason why the property, together with the island of Tiree, is in the market. The castle, whichle beautifully situated on the right bank of the River Bray, has long been the seat of the Campbells, of whonsil the Duke of Argyll is chief, but, as the property has teen advertised in the New York papers, it seems not unlikely that it will pass into the hands of some American millionaire. Scotsmen generally will regret the circumstance, should such a fate befall Inverary Castle, and there will be • similar feeling in England, if another historically lemons place—Battle Abbey— should pass into the hands of an alien. Just now, when so many people are travelling on their holidays it is as well to remember that the liability of railway companies for lost or stolen luggage, is not un- limited. A lady recently lost her dressing bag, after leaving it in charge of a porter at one of the Londo stations, and she claimed for the full value, which was declared to be a large sum. It was however, decided that tinder the Carriers Act, she could not recover more than £lO, not having previously declared its value, before giving the bag into the otsaree of the railway company's servant. It is therefor 3 advisable to keep valuable dressing bags, in ow's own poFeession, or else declare their value before handing them over to the cw- tody of railway employees. For country and seaside wear, there is. happily, SOMP evidence of a reaction against the elaterate and ex- travagant frocks, which are both costly and uncom- fortable. For freshness and daintiness,shere is nothing better than the cool looking linens, in pale and becom- ing shades, and the tendency is, to return to these costumes, made with a studied simplicity of style and ornament. Children also should only be put into the more inexpensive materials, and in hot weather little girls seldom look better than in coats and skirts of bright blue linen. Shirts and fronts under smart coats in cool weather ; and entire costumes of foulard and muslins, linens and grenadinee, for the hot season, have between them, ousted all blouses, save those which are worn with a skirt with which they perfectly accord. This renunciation of:contrasts between bodice and skirt brings us nearer to the Princess gown,always favoured by Frenchwomen and cut by their workers in a way few B itish fingers can imitate. There are varieties and modificatioos, for a dress fastening straight down from neck to heels, is not elaborate enough for us nowadays. Therefore must the bodice book over the arm, round at the back, atd across the front, as intricately as poc- sible, its point of fixed junction with the skirt being at the back only. Neat narrow belts are liked, the triple I row of meeting and parting ribbon., fastening under a rosette, being always desirable. Many of the newest bodices dispense even with there, and have a depth below the waist, and an inclination to little tails at the back, that suggest the return of the old time " jacket bodice." Straw bat shapes are receiving rough treatment from the milliners just now. Deliberately are they sliced with the knife at regular intervals round the base of the crown, and through the gashes a chiffon scarf is threaded. Large rush or " rumpumpus " bats for young gins are delightful when inflicted with a douta'o row of these spaces, one set alternating with the other. Through one row of holes is run a scarlet, through the other a rich yellow cord, and these Spanish colours blend at the side with loops and ends. Even the muslin " baby" flop h its suffer from the rage for lacings, and are threaded through and through with coloured ribbon or, better stil/, with narrow velvet. Chintz is returning to faveur,and the older generation is wondering why it was ever discarded. It is clean - wearing, tough, sad washes eptendiely. Sickly green and blue tapestries replaced it, and it is significant thst the old material reappears net in the (so-galled) " art " designs and colors, but in good bold blues, reds, end greens, and in really handsome designs that are neither ambitious nor unmeaning. Chintz has been restored to us for furniture covers, and for h- ngings, and a day may come when the appropriate makes of it may supply our summer gowns, as they did those of our grand- mothers. " MADGE "</p>
        <p>CHAPTER I. IN WHICH I ' EIRE A PURPLE SCAR.</p>
        <p>Down came the curtain on the first act ,of "The Bells" at the Lyceum. I had sat spell-bound, for never before had I seen Sir Henry Irving, never before had I been inside a London theatre.</p>
        <p>"Isn't he glorious ?" I said to my mother, with an ecstatic sigh. She did not answer; ard, glancing quickly into her face, I saw that she I, 'I not heard me. There was a strange, blank look her great dark eye.— eyes that, when I was a chid, I used to say were "like wells of ink, with stars at the bottom"—as if ler spirit had strayed from her body and forgotten the way back again.</p>
        <p>I touched her in a scared, hesitating way, as else wakes a person who has cried out in a dream. °Mother—mother dear " I whispered. "What's the matter, Sheila ? " slur asked, dazedly. "Oh, nothing, dear," I hastened to say. "I was afraid that something was the matter with you. You—you looked so odd. Are you quite well ?" "Yes," she answered. "Sir Henry Irving's face, in his make-up for Matthias, reminded me of—a wan I used to know, and haven't seen for—a very long time, that's all. And thoughts came up out of the past." "A man you'd rather not think of ? " I ventured, encouraged that she should answer my first question; for she did not always answer questions. Her lips tightened. For a moment she did not • Then she said, slowly: "It disturbs me to of him. I wish you had chosen some other play for your birthday night." "I wanted to see this one so much l " I exclaimed. "And I thought you wanted it, too. But if you'd rather go home--" "No, no," she broke in, with a note of impatience in her voice. "It is nothing at all—a mere fancy. Please forget about it, Sheila, and be as happy as you can. Your very first visit to a London theatre, child! Remember, there can never be another first time."</p>
        <p>Everything that was happening to me lately was happening for the first time. I had just COMO to London; I had just been presented; I had just found out that I was a pretty girl, and that people rather liked to talk to me. To-night it appeared that they rather liked to look at me as well—when the curtaia was down, and there was nothing better to do—for as I peeped out between the draperies of the box a great many opera-glasse; were lifted. _ _ _</p>
        <p>My first impulse was to draw back, self- sonsciously ; but it was my ambition to seem quite a woman of the world (it usually is, when one is eighteen), and I decided to appear oblivious to the fact that I was a centre of attraction. In a moment I actually became so; for two persons who were gazing up at the box caught my attention, and caused me to forget my important little self. One was a young man in the pit—a young man neither well-dressed nor remarkably handsome; yet, as I glanced at him, under cover of my lashes, I felt that his was a face to pick out in a crowd. If there were a fire in the theatre, and he said that he- would save me, I would unhesitatingly trust him to do it. He was not at all the sort of young man I had ever known, or was likely ever to know. Yet, as our eyes met for an instant, a thought-wave seemed to pass from his spirit to mine. It was not a mesge ; it was a sensation; I can describe it by no other word. But I knew that if I had been born in his sphere, or he in mine, we must have meant something in one another's lives. I wondered if the same impression was in his mind ; his eyes said yes ; and, with the blood rushing hotly to my cheeks; I booked away, feeling some- how guilty and ashamed, as if I had spoken to a stranger. Turning from him, I encountered other eyes fixed upon me with a very different expression. A woman had come in late, and had just taken a seat in the stalls, in 911C11 a position as to command an excellent view of our box. She was dressed in black satin, glittering with a great deal of jet, and a large diamond comb gleamed like a crescent moon in her black hair, streaked with grey. The light shone full upon her aquiline features, as she stared fixedly up at our box, and her eyes, enormously large, though deep-set, looked yellow- grey, and luminous as a cat's, in contrast with the thick, short black lashes and olive skin. The woman fastened these curious, pale eyes upon me for a long moment, with an extraordinary, almost greedy, interest; then they travelled from me to mother, who was leaning back in her chair, listlessly reading the naines on the. programme. • If it had not been ridiculous to imagine such a thing, I should have fancied deliberate malice in the look fixed upon that beautiful, sad face. I drew my chair a little further back, and began asking mother questions about the various actors and actresses. Until the curtain went up on the next act I kept her attention concentrated upon me; but when the lights were down throughout the house, except on the stage, I could not resist the unpleasant fascinatiOn that the dark face with the pale cat-eyes had already begun to exercise over my spirit:</p>
        <p>I glanced once more at the shabby young man in the pit; but this time he was absorbed in the play, and I experienced a slight, indefinable sense of resentment that he no longer thought of me. I, too, turned to the stage, and speedily forgot the impertinent gipsy-face in the stalls, until the curtain fell, and I remembered her with a start. I think it must have been that her eyes had inten- tionally drawn mine, as if with all her force she had willed me to think a her again ; for she was watching nie, and slowly, very slowly, drawing off a long black suede glove. Her sleeves only reached the elbow, where they ended in a fall of thick jet fringe; and as she removed the glove, her large, well-shaped arm lay like a column of marble on the black satin of her gown. At last she turned her arm so that the under part, with the palm of the hand, lay uppermost. Half-way between the elbow and the wrist was a purple patch, about the size of a common pansy, but of a different shape; and a horrid thrill went through my nerves as I sharply realised that the woman's action was wholly for me. She was taking off her gloves that I might see the scar. Others might notice what was happoning ; for this she did not care. The silent message was for our box, and nowhere else.</p>
        <p>In any circumstances, there must have been something uncannily disagreeable about this ; but I knew, and the woman knew (I felt sure), that which made it worse than disagreeable... . .</p>
        <p>The, mark on her a-rm to which she wished to attract my attention was shaped like a heart; and it was not the first of the kind which I had seen. Such a scar had made, tor years, all of mystery that my life had ever known. I had thought of it by day, I had dreamt of it by night; I had once been severely punished for asking questions con- cerning it; for there was such a purple, heart- shaped scar—the very fellow of the one I now beheld—in the same position on the arm of my mother. As I looked down, with dilated eyes, m hands I tensely grasping the ledge of the box, heard myself utter a faint sound, between a gasp and a sigh. I would have given much if I could have taken it back ; but it was too late. Mother had heard, and was asking, in her quick, nervous way, what was the matter. "Oh—nothing, nothing!" I exclaimed, as she had said to nie a short time before. But I could not make my voice sound natural, and she leant out from the boa to see for herself what had drawn from me that one tell-tale exclamation. The white arm still lay on the black satin lap down below, and I saw my mother's eyes find the scar. I felt the start she gave, as if it had gone shuddering through my own body. With an unconscious movement , she seized my wrist, grasping it so tightly that I Could have cried out with pain. I had not dreamt that she was so strong. She stood up, dragging me also from my chair. "I must go—at once," she said. "I must see—that woman down there." "Let U 3 send for her to come and speak with you here," I said, soothingly. "I'll call an attendant— "No, no," mother answered, her voice sharp 'with impatience. "She wouldn't come to me. Don't you see she is going? If I don't follow at once I shall lose her, and all the old m' ry will have to be lived over again. Open the Loor for me, Sheila—open the door." She would have gone without her cloak, which had been flung over the back of her chair, if I had not caught it up And wrapped it round heir</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MT Damt NELLIE, There are few prettier materials than beige or biscuit-coloured voile, especially in the new kind, which is more like canvas than voile. A beige batiste was one of the successes at a recent gather- ing, simple as the material is. It had a flounce of Lace round the skirt, strapped on with a band of beige glace and headed by two similar bands. There was a bolero of the lace, with long ends, in front, and the sleeves and collar were finished with soft, creamy lace. It is now quite correct to com- bine soft and thick laces in one gown. Beige is now accepted as half-mourning, especially when worn with black. Nothing looks cooler than beige, not even pale green and white. Plain white is very glaring in bright sunshine, and it may be remarked in passing that the mothers who canopy their babies' perambulators with white are very cruel to their offspring, without knowing it. The babies lie back, and cannot get away from the glare, which is harmful to their eyes, even when they are closed in sleep. Holland colour is rest- ful, but if white must he used there should he a lining of pale green.</p>
        <p>Halt-length coats in linen or holland are liked for driving, or for putting on after bathing, when a quarter of an hour's brisk exercise is advisable while the hair dries. Holland ie preferable to tussore for a dust-cloak, for the for-er 'as some little stamina, even after a slight shower, whereas the latter is very easily dejected. In fact, it hardly keeps a drop off the dress beneath. The holland coats wash and do up beautifully, another , point in their favour. The frilled sun hat is now largely worn both in cycling and boating. When perched and pinned at the correct angle, these hats protect the eyes from the sun and are 5.9 daintily pretty a form of headgear as could he devised.</p>
        <p>Pate blue drill makes a smart gown for river at errantry fete wear, and a pretty example had the skirt strapped and stitched down the seams and the bodice cut sailor fashion, with collar of &amp;len embroidered linen and a shirt of white tucked silk. A tie of soft pale blue silk was knotted under the collar, and the belt was of white glace. The hat that accompanied this gown was of ecru straw, flat and drooping of brim, the trimming consisting of bfne forget-me-note, ecru lace, and white ghie ribbon very deftly combined. Striped blue or green and white cambric is another cheap material that makes up smartly if a little trouble is taken over it. The skirt, for instance, can have a deep tucked flounce headed by a band of cream ()rocket lace, and braces of the same trimming may be placed on either side of the bodice, the vest being of tueked cambric. The full sleeves should have tight lace cuffs, and the belt should be of rather narrow satin ribbon tied in a bow at the side of the front. The floral patterned cottons look well made up with large sailor of plain-coloured hues' edged with guipure or crochet, or they may be effectively trimmed with strappings of white or cream linen, or washing.ribbon is sometimes need.</p>
        <p>Bathing cost:lmes are now beginning to occupy attention. A smart one in white serge is pleated on bodice and ti kii t, and has a scarlet linen collar turned bark from the vest, also scarlet. Some of the new bathing dresses have bol4ros, real or stimulated, in braid or braiding. Many of the French nati.int; costumes are not meant for bath- ing in, and it would be a mistake to copy their elaboration wen real business is intended with Father Nep:une, But simplicity, on the other hand, may be carried too tar, as in the hideous sacks in which many Englishwomen still continue to bathe. A happy medium is found in the weN- cnt serge suit consisting of knickerbockers and tunic with short sleeves, dark blue bound with wide red or white braid, or scarlet with nalry blue. For children, thick galatea is a good material, but it is not very suitable for their mothers or elder sisters. Your loving JENNIZ.</p>
