      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>Liverpool and District League. Warrington V. Wigan.</p>
        <p>There wig a large attendance of the public at the Clrporation Batts, L7gh-strret, on Tuesday evening, when those teams met for the first time this season in C nnection with the above competition. The inclusion Ito he evrning's prfigramme of a Juni or Handicap for boos was doubtless a source of attraction to the youth- ful community, and a fair number of entries had been secured. There were four beats, and four in each heat, The iwinnors of the heats were:—Asbton, Woods, Wain- wright and Woolstencroft. The final aroused consider- able enthusiasm and, after an excellent race, resulted in a win for Wainwright, Woods being second, and Ashton third. In the polo match the teams lined up as follows : Warrington. Goal. Robertson • full-backs, Boscow and W Ashton •, half-bulk, C As'hton ; forwards, Lowe, Hughes and Sutton. Wigan. Goal, Smith; full-barks, Sauudets and Midhews ; half-back, Swift ; forwards KearPley, Peacock and Magrath. Mr J Finnegan (Liverpool) was the referee. Warrington took the seven foot end. In the early tat-,,es of the game Robertson was tested, but had no difficulty in clearing, play being takso to the centre. From an excellent passing moved merit by the Wig k n forward 7 F Beanies, sent in a gran- shot which gave Robertson little chance. Any doubt as to the go'il was set at rest by air F nnegan awarding the requisite point. F mis were frequent, and at half- time the score was one to nil in favour of Wigan. On resuming. the Warrington forwards made a dash, the struggle in the goal mouth ending in nothing tangible. Wigan again asantned the aggressive,ald excellent Ilhoti4 by Swift and Saunders were successful,' dealt with by Robertson. No further score was recorded, and Wigan who had had the best of matters throughout, retircd vlC'ors by one goal to tone.</p>
        <p>Dining th, month of June the shinments of salt from the bfer.ey ports amounted to 59,892 tons, an increase of 8,408 tons compared with the corresponding month of last year.</p>
        <p>. The yield of dirt taxation in France for June shows a decrease of 14.592 200 francs am compared with the Budget estimates, while it is 27.345,800 franca below the amount collected in June, 1900.</p>
