        <p>KITCHEN FURNITURE, RASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS. (1. YEW kIIOP-EOILED ONES AT COST) HARROPS LIMIT6D, ARE THE LARCIESk HOUSE FURNISHERS, in the country and manufacture nearly every artide they sell. They supply goods on the easy payment eYetera cheaper than most houses do for cash. 1111</p>
        <p>11AXE A NOTE Ot THE ADDRES3 AND CALL</p>
        <p>fIARROPS LTD., 122, BRIDGE-STREET WARRINGTON, INSPECT OUR STOCK NO ONE PLELSED TO BCY "ICES GOODS OkFEEED AT SELL THICK.</p>
        <p>MESSRS PETER WALKER AND li SONS' EXCURSION. I</p>
        <p>Glorious Weather and a Successful</p>
        <p>Outing. It is with feelings of unalloyed pleasure that the Warrington employees of Messrs Peter Walker and Sons, the well-known firm of brewers, will look back upon this year's annual excursion, which took place last Saturday. Una atmospheri al conditions were ideal, and the outing passed off with unprece- dented success. Morecombe,the beautifully situated resort which has o justly earned the title of " The Naples of the North " wae again vieited.and thither the employees were conveyed in two i•pecial trains which left Bank Quay Stfilion shortly before 6 a.m. As usual the directors had issued free railway tickets to the workpeople and had else provided them with a liberal amount of pocket money, and needless to say, their generosity was fully appreciated. Upwards of 800 persons took part in the excursion. The morning broke cloudily, but by the time the trippers arrived at Morecambe the sun was shining brilliantly, and continued to do so for the remainder of the day. The Brewery band which has only been in existence for a year, accompanied the excursion and it must have been gratifying to tha management to see how smart the men appeared in their new uni- form of Forester's green with yellow facings. The uniforms, together with the splendid instrumente, and all tke expense of tuition have been generously paid for by the directors.Tbe band is a full brass one and is under the mastership of Mr P .1 Brady, who is a well•known musician in this town. The per- formances on Saturday were ieally a surprise to many, considering the short time the members have beent praetising and augur well for the future. Indeed it may be confidently expected that next season the public may have the pleasure of listening to their music in the Park and that "Walker's Brewery Band" may be recognised as one of the important musical institutions of the Borough. The band was **teat acquisition to the excursion and lent a feature of completeness to it which we congratulate the directors on having in- troduced. We wish the band all ,success. It was a matter of regret to all that the popular man- ager,(Mr 3 Fulton Smith)was prevented by serious indisposition from accompanying the excursion. A telegram was received from him expressing the hope that the trip would be a success. On arriving at More- cambe the excursionists dispensed to enjoy themselves in a variety of ways. The railway ticket was the pass- port of admission to the Central Pier and Winter Gardens, and at each of these places a capital entertain- ment was provided. Many of the more youthful trippers indulged in rowing, whilst the numerous attractions on the sands were the source of pleasure to others. The directors bad also arranged for the employees to haves Sail to Grange-ever-sands, and two special steamers had been chartered. The sail was a most enjoyable experi- ence. Morecambe looks at its best when viewed from the sea, and the unusually clear atmosphere enabled the passengers to obtain a glimpse of Blackpool Tower and Great Wheel. The Cumbrian mountains could also be clearly distinguished and Grange-over-Sands bathed in the glorious sunshine looked charming. On returning a number of the excursionists went by tram to the ancient town of Lancaster, where they inspected the historical castle and church. Others drove to the pictur- esque village of Heysham aed to the artillery camp at Bare. In the evening the heads of the firm together with a number of invited guests dined at the King's Arms Hotel. A capital spread was provided, and after dinner the usual toast list was gone through. Mr James Monro presided and the vice-chair was occupied by Mr John Baird. In proposing the toast of " Peter Walker end Son Warrington and Burton) Limited), Alderman Fran- omb said he believed that all rresent would agree with him that the firm were excellent employers of labour, and he did not think that any, from Mr Monro to the smallest office boy would ever be forgotton if he only did his duty. (Applause). The man who did his duty to' the firm of Peter Walker and Son would never fail L .) be /ewarded. They were also most liberal to what- ever towns they were connected with. In Liverpool one of the haest buildings which that city possessed' was the Walker Art Gallery, which was erected through the generosity of the late Sir Andrew Walker. No morewhich generousas ts ma ever lived, and be believed his sons would follow i n broughtlli s footsteps their and notice. assist any p (Applause). ). causel In Warrington they had a beautiful fountain which was a great addition to the Park. Their hospitality was also very great, and he was sure that everyone who was pre- sent that night would appreciate it, especially as it was given most ungrudgingly. He could only hope that their success would continue to be so great that they would be able to afford to give them this treat yearly. (Hear, hear and applause). There was someone present who would propose the health of Mt Monro, but be might say that if ever they had the right man in the right place that man was Mr Monro. (Hear. hear and applause). The Chairman responded. Mr W. 0. J. JENKINS proposed "Success to the town and trade of Warrington." Referring to the Corpora- tion he said that at the present time the members of that body were up to the neck in hard work, but he was afraid they Were not getting very much thanks for what they did. They all hoped they would live to attain their full reward, that the worn they were then engaged in and still bad to carry out would be rewarded by a triumphant success, and that they would teceive the full appreciation of the public. Be hoped they would forget and forgive altogether the little vicissitudes which corporate bodies and officials bad to put up with. Those who criticized the work ought to do so in a kindly spirit Then they would assist in bringing about that true measure of success which they all hoped and believed would be attained. (Applause.) Be coupled with the toast the name of Alderman Sutton, the "father of the Council." Applause.) Alderman Sunox,in returning thanks,said he assured the company that the members of the Council deserved • all the good things which had been said about them. (Hear, hear, and laughter ) He must tell them that the position of a member of the Town Council of Warrington was, at the present time, no sinecure. It was a position of great importance. A member could only represent his part of the town at great personal sacrifice. At the present moment the Town Council of Warrington was composed of members who did their duty as well as any memberelhad:done since the incorporation of the borough Tkeres was a good deal of criticism with regard to their expenditure. The staple industry of the town—the iron trade—at the present time was in a very depressed COO- dition, but things were improving and he hoped they would continue to improve. He was afraid that this de- pression had been caused by the workmen themselves. When there was good trade and work to be had they would not work as long as they might, and thus enable the manufacturers to take in orders. He hoped, how- ever, that things would cocticue to improve (Applause). Several vocal items were submitted and the proceed-</p>
        <p>InxA terminatEd with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>Subsrquently a visit was paid to the Winter Garden Pavilion where seats had been reserved for the company. The return journey was commenced shortly befote eleven o'cLck, and Warringtou was reach dat 12 45, after an emineLtly succasaful excursion.</p>
        <p>EVIDENCE Nov EIEARSAY.—A careful investigation has ben made by a trustworthy reporter into a remark- able cure in Manchester ascribed to the use of Dr Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. The whole affair has been sifted out, and an interview wit' t the person concerned obtained, with the result that the Case is found an astonishing one. The evidence collected by the n porter is submitted in another column. Froth what we can learn, LAncashire swear by Dr Williams' Pink Pills, and it is not to be wondered at in view of the hundreds of cures they have effected in cases of paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and sAatica, also all diseases arising from itnpoverishinent of the blood, scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels ar.d lungs, ai teania, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, loss of appetite, palpita- tions, pains in the back, nervous headache and neuralgia, early decay, all forms of female weakness, and hysteria. These pills are a tonic, not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name—Dr Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People—and are sold by chemists and by Dr Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn- viaduct, London, E.C. at 2i 9.1 a box or six boxes for 139 91. Pink Pills said loose or from glass jars are not Dr Williams' Pink Pills. Accept them only in the pink closed wrapper as above described.</p>
