      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS ENCAMPMENT. Departure To-day.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEERS ENCAMP- MENT. Departure To-day.</p>
        <p>To-day (Saturday) the local volunteers will leave Warrington for Perham Downs, where the' 4 will form part of a Brigade Camp. The Fatigue Party.</p>
        <p>The advance or fatigue party paraded at the Drill Hall, at eleven o'clock on Tuesday night, undPr the command of MajorJ D Buckton.to entrain for Salisbury Plain. The party consisted of Quanermaster and Hon. Lieutenant A F G Potter. Qaartermaater-Sergeant J Wilicock, Arm.-Sergeant er Davies, one sergeant, and 21 rank ana file, and a transport detachment of one ser- geant and nine men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Sunday's Church Parade.</head>
        <p>Sunday's Church Parade.</p>
        <p>There was an excellent turn-out at the annual church parade of the let Volunteer Battalion, Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment). prior to the visit to camp on Sunday morning. The men, to the number of from 500 to 600, left the Drill Hall at about ten o'clock for the Parish Church, the officers in command being Major Buckton, Major and Adjutant Roome, Captains Appleton, J D Fairclough, Croefleld, Smith, Cary (who returned from South Africa on Saturday) • Lieutenants Will- cocks and Roberts, and Second-Lieutenants Ridg- way, Avery, and Haste= The procession was headed by the Regimental Band under the direction of Bandmaster Richard- son; next came the bugle band under the superin- tendence of Sergeant-Bugler Plinston, the Mounted Infantry, the cyclist company and the main body of the regiment. The men looked very smart, and their appearance was favourably commented upon by the large crowds, who evinced great interest in the proceedings. Seats were reserved for the men in the body of the church, and there was a large congregation, which included the • Mayoress and Miss Roberts. The prayers were read by the Rector and the Revs A S Cameron, and G W Dickinson,and the lessons by the Rector, and the Rev E J Will- cocks. Mr F A Darby, Mus Bac., Oxford, presided at the organ, but the accompaniments for the hymns—" All people that on earth do dwell,' "Fight the good fight," 'The Church's one founda- tion" and the National Anthem were supplied by the Volunteer Band. The sermon was preached by the Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis) from the words " Behold to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams," 1 Sam. ay 22. Toe Rector said he had often spoken to the men as Volunteers,. hut he wished to speak to them that morning as men, as individuals, as reaular attend- ants at worship on the Lord's Day in the House of Goal. Was that a supposition? Was the latter part of the sentence • supposition ? Very earnestly and effectually he ventured to ask them to ask themselves " If I were not a Volunteer should he in the MAW/ of GO tu-day ?' He intended to speak to them no,. simply as Volunteers and soldiers—although he should say a few words to them from that point of view later on—but as men, as individuals, who, he earnestly trusted would in time, if not now, be regular attendants in the House of God on the Lord's Lay. He then went on to say that one great lessson to be learnt was obedience to authority. The men he was addressing were soldiers; they bad proved themselves to be soldiers during the past year. They were recoguizsd now not only as Volunteers but as soldiers, and as soldiers they knew the importance cf obedience. No man was fic, to command until be bad learnt to obey. A soldier or sailor had to obey under all circumstances The second lesson be wished to draw was that of true. religion. " Behold to obey is better than sacrifice and to ‘learken than the fat of rams." It was a good thing for the Volunteers to have come to church that day to offer up the sacrifice of worship to God, but if their hearts were not in the servic,-, then music was as " a tinkling cymbal" and had no sound in the ears of Gad. Why should a man be ashamed of religion ? Why should he be ashamed of God and His Church ? Ought he not to be brave? Everybody respected courage,and a good and brave man. The return to the Drill Hall was aritnesg.d by a stil larger crowd.</p>
      </div>
