      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FIE EXA ivilNElt, .FRU AY, JULY 12 1901</head>
        <p>FIE EXA ivilNElt, .FRU AY, JULY 12 1901</p>
        <p>LABOJIR CON DI noNs.</p>
        <p>[By Mr. Henry Whitham]. I gather trom myt " Examiner," that the path of the British workman is not altogether one of peace, Ile has one consolation, he is not alone in the struggle he is carrying on to better his surround- ings. Never in all my experience have I seen such a state of things as exists now and has done for a number of months in San Francisco Everything has been organ.zed front the street sweepers, dish washers, shoe blacks, teamsters right up to the aristocratic engine driver, and telegraphers. I ex- pect to d'ee the hoodlums on the streets next organ- izing, with the view of looking after their vested Interests. Never since I came to the States have I known each, a huge wave of prosperity pass over the land. Everything seems to be booming. and Ithe workman seems to be determined to have his share of the good things as they are passed along. This of course is leading to higher prices for everything. A cheap shave may be had for or the beard be trimmed for a paltry sd. A stre-t sweeper is willing to work eight hours a aley for 9s, while another man in the street will dEive a team of horses for 128 a day. With all this 'prosperity we are having lots of strikes. All over the country,the machinists are asking for a reduc- tion in hours with an increase in pay. Had this been all the men asked for itf would have been readily granted, but it •is in all cases insisted that ' the labour, organization shall be recognized, and that the agreements shall be drawn up by officials. , This latter clause has caused the employers to 'organise, not, as they say, to oppose unions or Union principles. On the contrary, theimajoritt Of employers say.:," We favour the unions, and we do not, blame the men who make a business of organinug labour, but we do not want to be told whom we shall and whom we shall not employ." The organizing and building up of a labour union is a mighty big job out here, and such as I never dreamed ot. Some two or three months ago a small group of stringers dropped suddenly upon the railway employees of San Francisco. Getting hold of a few prominent men among the workers an already printed Constitution was submitted. Trade unionism had been to much in the air I suppoie we; all caught the complaint right off, and when the first open meeting was called quite a crowd attended and planked down their 69. This meant 4s for the initiation and 2s for a month's dues paid beforehand. The ee.et thing, a local secretary must be appointed at a salary of £4 a week. There are now near on seven hundred members in the local division and organ izers are being sent all over the states. A general chairman and general secretary have been appointed who give their whole time to the business, and who will, I anticipate, receive a paltry thousand pounds or so, a year for their services. Truly trade union- ism in the United S:aces is a great thing. In all the other labour unions a leading feature is Federation, In every city will be found a branch of the American Federation of labor. This branch or Labour Council is made up of representatives from all the labour unions in a given district, and has a great deal of power vested in it. A grievance in any trade or calling is brought before the Council and tried upon its merits, and if the Council is satisfied a grievance really exists, a deputation is appointed to wait upon the employers. Sometimes a little trouble comes right here, as the large employers do not like to recognize these " strangers." A little see- saw work goes en,and suppose no I.micable arrange- ments can be made:a strike is declared. Then comes the fun. Some weeks ago a strike of the cooks and waiters was declared, and in San Francispo, this is no light affair when it is understood that a large majority oi all classes of workers take at least two meals a day at the restaurants. Some curious sights Dave been seen on the streets during this strike. Every restaurant that did not agree to put a union card in the window, and do business according to the house seen da nwnoo mu necni nwg otuol dt hpea cpea stsoe rasnbdy f trho a t ftohries the Union's requirements was boycotted. A number of is an unfair house don't patronise it, go a bit further on to a union house." This is carried on , all through the day and half through the night. Qaite a number of the houses have had to close not being able to get help. Another interesting feature is the sympathetic strike. A teamster will I haul no lumber unless it has on it the ' union stamp. He will haul no meat to a ' non union restaurant, nor will a union scavenger ' take away the refuse of such a house. A business 'man not being in sympathy with this endless chain of boycotting, may wish to put an end to it by re- fusing supplies to a manufactarer who has made himself obnoxious by taking part with the union- ists. In this case the endless chain is set working and the business man becomes aware of a hornet's nest he has run against. The teamster drives up for the supplies, and they must be forthcoming or all the warehousemen will walk out. lam trying to describe the trade unionism of the United States. It is one of the tine arts, and were I to fill the whole of the " Examiner " I should still leave a deal untold. Now, it will be clear from the above how chaotic things would soon become, were the railway em- ployes,through their unions,to become members of the Federated Trades. If a non-union teamster wished to deliver any non-union freight. or even should he have a union made coffin to deliver we should have to refuse the lot. The- reader's imagination can follow this endless chain of boycotting. It is enough to say that the railway employee in building up their union have kept clear, as yet, of the possibilities of bringing about the complete disruption of busi- ness relationship. The railway organization now being formed, and in which I am taking an humble part, seems to me to be taking a complete depar- ture from the usual lines of trade unionism, In ethics it seeks to encourage its members to attain a higher standard of morality, especially as regards gambling,the use of intoxicating beverages of every character, and all vicious habits. This in itself is a big order, as in this city of San Francisco drink- ing and gamb!ing are, without doubt, the two most prominent features in the daily life of the workman. Gambling! Why everybody gambles, or nearly so. Men and women go on year after year spending their dollar a month on lottery tickets, and yet one seldom hears of their winning anything. As to the whisky drinker it eems to be an article in the religion of almost every workman that he must have a " livener " before he starts work, one at neon before he can eat, and I know not how many at evening before he can sleep,and bear inmind each drink costs sd. Kxperience has tauglv, most men who are interested in the labour movement, that nothing will ever be achievedby the workman until he gets rid of these two of his greatest failings— gambling and drinking. It has always been an accepted feature in labour organizations that one who bad authority in any line of business, could not become a member of a union. This feature we are departing from, and strange as it may appear to the English reader we are doing so because we have a successful example of its working set before us ie the working pf the amalgamated society of railway servants of Eng- land, Ireland, Scotland and Wales, The superin- tendent of a District, the foreman of the freight sheds are all eligible for membership. To me, this appears like a foolish attempt to make men in authority over other men serve two mas- ters. To make this plainer, can a foreman meet with his men in a club-room, and hear them dis- cuss a question of more pay,and shorter hours and be faithful to his employer if he does not report what he has heard, and be faithful to his brothers in the union if he does report all he h -ars ? This is a question over which I have had a severe struggle, and in one very large meetirg a resolution was actually put that I be expelled because I failed to anticipate the millenium. Another feature of this new twentieth century labor movement, and on similar lines to the above, has had all my poor critical powers bestowed upon it, but without success. I cannot for a moment accept the propo- sition that men who are in receipt of wages runn- ing from 2.50 dollars to 100 dollars a month will throw in their lot wit'-, men who are only earning 40 and 50 dollars a month. Men are not yet so angelic as to join a strike to benefit their poorer brothers. I should take it as a great favour, if any reader of the " Examiner," knowing anything of the workings of the British railway union can give me any facts or eximple9 as to the working of the altruistic features recited above. Well, I need not dwell any more on this new labour movement, more than to say that the hope of the organizers is to have within a few months a society of at least a hundred thousand members,and then 1 guess it is likely the world will see anew de- velopment in the labour and capital questions. All this organizing of labour is simply the out- come of what we see going on in the world of capital. When a Trust can be formed with a presi- dent at its head who is receiving a yearly salary of 1,000,000 dollars a year, it makes workmen think. FL WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>1318, Ruerrero-street, Ban Francisco.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>STRANGE TALE OF A MANCHESTER HOSPITAL. --- Out -Patient's Treatment. Her Husband Seen.</head>
        <p>STRANGE TALE OF A MAN- CHESTER HOSPITAL. --- Out -Patient's Treatment. Her Husband Seen.</p>
        <p>An affair has come to light at Halms within the last few days, which is regarded in the neighbourhood with quite extraordinary interest. A Manehester reporter ran across the incident in question and obtained an exclusive story. Mrs Nizsbeth Harvey, forty-ene years of age, resides at No 45. Owen-street, flulme, Manchester, and the reporter interviewed her a few a ays ago at her residence., where aloe kindly related the facts which follow. Exact accuracy being, as our readers will observe, of some importance. Mrs Harvey's exact words are given as taken down by the reporter. " I have turned forty-one years of age," she said, " and I have been troubled with a cough and chronic rains in the chest ever since I can remember. The doctors said it was bronchitis. I got so bad that three years ago I was induced to I attend the Hospital for Consumption and Diseases of 1 the Throat and Chest,in Ilardman-street, Deansgate, as les outpatient. The least exertion made me very short of breath, and I was many times unable to attend to ,my household duties. I became very weak, as I had no desire to eat, and did not enjiy my food. The rack- -1 ing cough was very distressing, and the pain in my &amp;lest was often almost unbearaele. In fact, I was al- together in a very miserable conditicn. The doctors examiaed me and prescribed for my complaint. They gave me medicine and supplied me with linament to 1 rub on my chest." you continue to take their medicaments 2" " Indeed I did ; I took a lot of medicine, but it did me no good. 1 got:no better, and I had the same pains 'in my chest. I got tired of going to the hospital, for the treatment did not benefit me at all ; at any rate I got verc little relief. At times, I was so short of breath that my family became alarmed for fear 1 should die of suffocation."</p>
        <p>-------- " And then what did you dc,, for you seem alright now."</p>
        <p>"It is now over two years ago that a Mr Grierson, whom I knew, and who had himself received great good from taking Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, pre- vailed upon me to try a box. I had not much faith in them at first, but I thought I would give them a fair trial. I went on taking them regularly. The first and second boxes did not produce any very tangible results any more than the hospital medicine,but after the third box I experienced a decided change for the better." " What was the nature of the change they iffectt d ?" " My cmgh became easier, and the pains in my chest gradually went away. I also got stronger every day, became more cheerful, a..:cl could go about my household duties with more pleasure. Work then became no trouble to me." _ _ ..... I</p>
        <p>"Row long did you continue taking Dr WillLm pink pills ? " "I have taken them at intervals ever since. I am taking them now. Whenever I feel that I require them I take one or two, and lam better in beak° at the present time than I have been for years." "And you are willing that your experiences should be made public?"</p>
        <p>" Certianly." "Do von know anyone else who has benefited by taking Dr Williams' pink pills ?" " Only Mr. Grierson, and my husband, and other people that I have read abcut in the newspaper 3."</p>
        <p>From photograph</p>
        <p>Man.chester.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Harvey, who is now a stout lady, and looking anything but a consumptive, then busied herself with her household duties, and the interview terminated. THE HUSBAND'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Later in the thy, when Mr. Abraham Harvey, the husband, came borne from his work at one of the cotton mills, which have been gradually decreasing in number in the city during the last quarter of a century, he was questioned, and made the following statement : What my wife has told you of her cure is quite true, and I can tell you something almost as interesting of myself. lam forty-four years of age, and an operative cotton spinner. I was bred and born in Hulme, and Owen-street and its neighbourhood have been my home over since. About eighteen months ago I began to be very ill. The particular form of illness that I suffered from I am unable to tell you, but I seemed to be broken up. I had no appetite, and consequently no bodily strength. At intervals I used to feel an acute pain between the shoulders, and particularly under the left shoulder blade. I even feel it now at times, but only slightly. On my way borne from work the pains in my back were so bad that I could scarcely get my breath. I used to pretend to be lacing my boots in the street in order to regain my breath and secure a moment's rest. I consulted a local doctor, and put my- self under his treatmem,but though he treated me moat skillfully for months, I bad to leave my weak. Getting no better, and being in despair, I asked the doctor if he thought a change of air would do me any good. He replied that be thought it would, and suggested South- port as a suitable place. Being in ray Trade Society was enabled to go to a convalescent hospital in South-1 port for three weeks. The doctor there examined me, and gave me medicines. He told me I should have to be very careful, as I was suffering from a very nasty complaint, but did not tell me what it was. However, neither medical treatment nor the. air of Southport revived me. In fact I got worse. Before I left I had lost six pounds in weight, and I was very weak. My spirits went down, and I seemed to bays no hope of getting better. I did not care whether I lived or died. I bad given up my work at the factory about three months before I went to Southport. After I had been home a few days I again went to my doctor. He ordered me home at once and said I was not to leave the house. I went on with hie medicine and in a few weeks ventured to crawl out of the house and get about a bit* I told the doctor that when I took a glass of bitter beer I could eat a bit of bread and butter,and asked him whether it was wrong forme to take the beer. He said it was not wrong to take it if I could eat aith it, but on the contrary it would be a good thing for me. So I continued to take the beer for a time, but my appetite again fell off, and I was °ace more in despair. It was then that my wife said 'Try Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people,' but I did not think they would do me any good, in fact, I was out of sorts with everything, and believed I should never get to work again. However, to please my wife I sent for a box of Dr Williams' pink pills, and began to take them." "And how long was it before you began to feel any better." "Not long, I kept getting a bit better, and after the second box I went on mending every day. I may tell you it was Sunday night; when I began to take Dr Williams' pink pills. I went to Mr A Ramsay, the druggist, in City-road, to buy them, and I have continued to buy them at the same place ever since. Be knew I had been ill_ for a long.time,and</p>
        <p>has asked me if they had benefitted we. I told him they had done me a great deal of good. I have kept on with them. I am now eating well, and have been at my work at the cotton mill three months. I get stronger every day. Indeed I may say lam quite well." " And you attribute your recovery solely to Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people." . . _</p>
        <p>"There's no "doubt about i. They have also saved my wife's life. That I can vouch for."</p>
        <p>.." And you are willing that these facts should be pub lished 2"</p>
        <p>" Yes, certainly ' • and my w;fe will be glad to have her story published also ?" "Do you know anyone else whe has benefitted by taking Dr Williams' pink pills ?" " Yes,there's the man who first asked me to try these pills, telling me that his son was taking them, and that they were doing him a power of good. I have since recommended them to another of my shop mates and they have done him a lot of good. I have told many a score of people about Dr Williams' pink pills. I (have gained a lot in weight lately." The reporter congratulated Mr Harvey on his personal appearance and good looks and the efficacy of the pills about which he had spoken so enthusiastically-.</p>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMP.N. RUNCORN. I On Sunday the anniversary rvices in connection with the above mission were held. The preacher in the morning was the Rev N J Poole, curate of Holy Trinity.Runcorn,and in the evening the pulpit WAS occupied by the Rev S H Wingtield-Digby, chaplain to the Mersey Mission, Birkenhead. A Children's service was held iu Midafternoon, when the Rev S H Wingfield-Digby, who had come down the Ship Canal on the mission launch Good Cheer, gave an interesting address to the children. The preacher's brother,Nlr, J K D Wingfield-Digby, UP, for North Dorset, read the lessons in the evening. ' The congregations were fairly good and the collec- tions satisfactory. On Saturday evening the usual open-air concert was given on the recreation field off the Holloway, by the Westen Works band. There were a good number present, and the even- ing was spent in a pleasant manner.</p>
