        <p>1</p>
        <p>a is ten years or mote' The Education Bill, since the country awoke to the necessity ef second' ary educktion, under the contemplation of the results which the superior system of commercial schools in. Germany had effected in giving, the natives of that country untold advantages over our own countrymen in the keen competition for the trade and commerce of the world. A Royal Commission was appoirated,and in 1894, after long and.careful investigation, it brought in an unani- mous report; but all these ye ars the I:nionist Government have failed to grapple with the ques- tion. Sir Jowl. Goasr has claimed that they now found their proposals upon the report of that Commission. But this is not the case. The sue' pression of the School Boards was never contera- plated by the Commission. At least, it found place in their report. What the Commission did was to recommend a composite authority, in which the School Board should share the. representa- tion and responsibility for secondary education equally with the municipal authorities. The proposal was conciliatory, and might have led t° an harmonious and prompt set tlement of the guo'. tion, which is most desirable; indeed, the conjen' tore may be hazarded that had it been adopted t"." the basis of the Government scheme the opposition now being so strenuously offered would never been heard of. But what they have done, as the tried to do in 1896, is to depart deltberately fronl the recommendation of the Commission, and h). putting the municipal councils in the foreground with the full purpose, of malice prepense, to. sopa' sede the School Boards altogether, to introduco? cause of contetabon where none ought to exist. In the resumed debate on the second reading of th: second bill in the House of Commons on Tuesdal evening, Mr. ERNEST GRAY, the Conservativ,e member for West Ham,who said some plain thing:is to the Government, remarked how strange it W, that when they met in the Committee roo't upstairs all parties agreed over thPse matters, ha a 9 I whenever the same things came before the 1:107,55, of Commons there was nothing but strife and n'be sension. This is a statement of fact, and it will tbst !seen who is to blame when iti pointed OlAt</p>
        <p>Upstairs every consideration was given to the educational authorities created by the Act ut 1870, while in introducing the Government rueasures it has been frankly admitted that the Purpose of the Government is to raze to the foundations the system established in 1870. This blazing faggot which has been so recklessly thrown into the Parliamentary barn in the dog- days of the session is the Government's contribu- tion to the cause of secondary education after</p>
        <p>aeven years' consideration. M3antirne German</p>
        <p>e.,lPorts have increased by 50 per cent. In another ueeede,'growing at the same rate, they will ex- need our own, and Great Britain will have receded horn her former supremacy to the third place 4lnong the nations engaging in foreign trade. The 6,llPerb organisation of trade and technical schools In Germany has been going on and improving for three generations. Our competitors are not con- tent with what exists; they are incessantly en- gaged b perfecting their equipment. They do no lull down and paralyse; nor do they supersed the good without having the better to put in its Place. But improvement and extension are con- tinuous, and every day the immense leeway It creases which we have to make up if we are ever to recover our trade supremacy, or even teeintain an equal footing with competing Powers, anch as Germany. The country may well awaken to the serious loss it is sustaining at the hands of laise stewards, who have but jeers and gibes for present educational methods, and have nothing better to propose in their place</p>
        <p>It seems that it will not The Protestant De- be easy to gratify the claration. sensitive feelings of Ro- blan Catholics in the matter of the declaration to</p>
        <p>.°Phold the Protestant faith which the Sovereign has to make on his accession to the Throne of great Britain and Ireland. After the debate in the Ouse of Lords on Monday afternoon the Govern- Slant must have the impression that they have Undertaken a rather ungrateful task. It was Understood that objection was taken merely to the Phraseology of the declaration, which it was alled contained certain offensive expressions which were gratuitously insulting to a portion of his MAJESTY'S subjects. The complaint seemed ralch a reasonable one that the Government under- 4(3°k the duty of removing the cause of offence; and now, when they have done so, they find that nobody approves of the result. The de- bate in the Upper Chamber revealed the curious tact that in this affair all parties are equally 'lagin the Government," and the report Would inevitably have been sent back to tile committee for reconsideration and amend- bleat had not the most reverend Primate who heads the bench of Bishops given a fresh Proof that among other latent qualities of soul he Possesses a wholesome terror of Cassar's power and a Prudent determination not to invoke the wrath !:/vl, the civil authority. He knew he had the 44, Ouse with him, but, nevertheless, he withdrew 4a motion, merely reserving his right to criticise 4.111 d amend the bill which it is said may be pro- as a result of the committee's report. The tllrious fact was revealed at the close of the dis- ion that, while it had been permitted by the tesy of the Lord Chancellor, it was altogether ef order, the proper procedure being to allow ti 8 report to lie on the table for future consid3ra- a24. The Chancellor, however. by waiving the ict forms of the Chamber for the convenience of Its members who were eager to unburden them- selves of their feelings in the matter, allowed a useful purpose to be served by means of the dis- cussion, which indicated the position of the various parties to the case. It is evident that the sigh Church party will join the Roman section 11:1 objecting not only to the phraseology of the d_eclaration but to the declaration itself. they now protest not against the insulting of :pecial rites, which have been termed idols- but against any of their peculiar tenets ,Qaing singled oat as incapable of being held by dle'British Sovereign. In feet, they would abolish entirely any declaration on the subject of religion /thatever. The Government must have been "rrare that this difficulty would confront them ; °Lit they are also aware that a large majority of ,th° Protestant inhabitants of the kingdom still rtand that there shall be no possibility of .°lslanism ever again lifting its head under the tirr'N'im of these islands. It is asked, glibly enough, should not the Kixo have the same liberty .."sr conscience regarding his religion as his subjects van claim ? But it is not a question of religion tilerely. The KING, as the head of the State and 0.0.0 of the Estates of the Realm, has a representa- tive duty committed to him of maintaining a sPecific attitude towards a system which is Political quite as much as—frequently far more than—it is religious. It is the Romish system Which the people of this country desire to inveigh against, ai.d a time when the baneful effects of that system are painfully apparent in every °°lllltry where it has obtained an ascendency is tlr't a time when the British people are likely to t'°Usent to any weakening of the protection Nztinst it which they seek to obtain through the cession declaration.</p>
        <p>0 Visitors to seaside places, /and especially those .11 the southern coast, are being warned to look 1;14 for that small but venomous fish, the lesser aver,which is reported this year to be in rather ;408ual numbers. The weever is by no means kegressive in its habits. It is only about three or Ur inches long, it has no pretensions to beauty ,;v4llcl it has a way of hiding in the sands in shallow 1 r.hate as if it were quite aware of its unpreposbess- • R appearance. A sharp back fin, however, pro- :lettB9 and is capable not only of inflicting a t_kther serious wound on the hands and feet Ituf. bathers, but also of poisoning the part 44ected. Of course, these lurking dangers je chiefly to be looked for on the lonely b'retches of coast affected by unconventional Ilthers, the only perils of the ordinary bathing- lia,ces being nothing worse than tiny crabs, unless Ct ne for an occasional medusa. And if swimmer diver should encounter one of these weevers,get 4w4ild or foot pricked, and become subsequently „ are of a throbbing pain in the part affected, we 1;44 Only advise him to follow the instructions given t? 4 writer in the " Morning Post," and lose no borae in applying a poultice as hot as it can be itir,l,?o. As a rule, if this treatment is undertaken I,ll"rne the throbbing ceases in an hour or two' and th goes well ; but there have been cases in which toe injury has been neglected, and amputation of 11 or of finger has been rendered absolutely ecessary. The prospectuses of the Public-House Trust in taPanies which have just been formed, or are Uer c'eess of formation, have been criticised not 4irsea,turally in regard to the smallness of capital shir..4,?t. Taking, for instance, the case of Ilamp- beo,:' 4-'25,000 appears to be thought all that is thePlry, or at least all that it is wise to ask, for `ntroduction of the new principle in the hold- thounf licences for a county which must contain arid Pads of public-house. At Southampton ortsmouth, to say nothing of other towns,</p>
        <p>there are scores of licenced houses the purchase of which alone would take up the whole capital of the company, both the public shares of £1 each which are to carry 5 per cent. dividends, and the twenty shares of is each which are to have 50 per cent, of the voting power, and convey the rest of the profits for public uses. The company, however, reserves the power to make a start if only £6,000 is subscribed. Then £50,000 is not much with which to attack the present licensing system in Kent, and other schemes show a like apparent insufficiency of capital. Even in places where, as in Sheffield, licenses acquired by the authorities in improvement schemes, &amp;c, will be handed over to the now companies, money will be required for building, and if this has to be borrowed the way is open for complications which may seriously handicap the movement from the start. Five per cent, is tolerably good interest on an investment as things go, and more capital might boldly have been asked for at the start. Either there should be more many behind the movement generally or it is a mistake to attempt at the moment to cover such an area as fifteen or twenty English counties and county boroughs.</p>
        <p>The account of "The Foreign Office from Within," which appears in the Empire Review " for this month, is at reminder that an Under- secretary who cannot answer supplementary ques- tions in the Commons may claim some indulgence. There are ten departments,of which five are purely political, and deal with Eastern arid Western Europe, Asia, Africa and America; two—the Librarian's and the treaty departments—are par- tially so ; while the financial, the consular and the commercial departments are non-political. Lord Lansdowne and Lord Cranborne are expected to keep in touch with all five, although only three concern them closely—and, of course, it is beyond the power of either of them to see more than a fraction of the correspondence which passes under their names. There are how- ever, a host of matters in regard to which they are exposed to a double criticism. The drafts of all official despatches on questions of international policy have to be submitted to the Sovereign, and although what passes between the Sovereign and his Ministers can only be hinted at, we have had recently indications that the inquiries are not merely nominal, Lord Salisbury, in his tribute to the late Queen Victoria, in giving praise to her sagacity spoke of the responsibility which a Minis- ter took in advising her Majesty to act agair,st her own judgment. The King is known to be still more active in giving to the Government the benefit of his information, all of which, while it is helpful, adds to the need for deliberation.</p>
        <p>October next will witness the jubilee of the erection of the first Wycliffe Congregational Church in Warrington. The present handsome church was preceded by a modest building which was opened for public worship on the same site fifty years ago. Prior to that period, meetings of Dissenters were held at the Nags Head Inn and other places in the town and district. The denomination has been worthily sustained in Warrington by a number of earnest and able pastors, amongst whom the Rev John Yonge occupies an honoured place. We understand that preparations are now in progress for the Jubilee celebrations. An effort will be made to include the ceremony of laying the foundation stones of the Rigby Memorial buildings as part of the proceedings. The need of suitable rooms for social and religious meetings has been greatly felt by the Church for a long time, and the new build- ings are intended to meet that want. They will also provide a suitable and well-deserved memorial of the late Mr and Mrs Samuel Rigby, of Bruche Hall, who did so much for Congregationalism in Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Warrington Town Council last week there was a resolution submitted by the General Purposes Committee which recommended the adoption of a scheme to provide a new War- rington Bridge over the Mersey in place of the existing one at a cost of, practically, £20,000. Let us say, here and at once, that the project was re- jected by 16 votes to 10. We are glad it was—unfeignedly glad. There is not the slightest doubt that a new bridge is needed, even urgently needed, in order that the great volume of traffic converging on it from different directions may be accommo- dated with safety and comfort. We do not sup- pose there are half a dozen people in Warrington who would not admit readily the need for a new bridge, yet nearly everybody endorses the action of the Council in rejecting the proposal to provide it straight away. Why? The question is easily answered: it 'son the ground of expense. This year has seen a sudden increase of 8d in the 2 on the rates, and, moreover, only a few weeks ago a scheme for Turkish baths at a cost of about £4,000 was dropped, after it had been approved. If it was expedient to shelve Turkish Baths, becam.se of the heavy expense the town is now on, surely it was sublime wisdom to do the same with War- rington Bridge, a scheme five times as costly. One argument in favour of the expenditure was that an additional penny rate would cover the cost. A penny in the pound is not a great deal, but coming on the top of Bd, it looks con- siderable. Then, too, there is the uncomfortable feeling in many minds that we have not seen tie last of increased rates and that tho commencement of the next financial year will witness a further call on the already depleted purse of the ratepayer.W hat justification there is for this foreboding remains to be seen, but as far as the average individual can form an opinion on such matters it certainly seems well-founded. Warrington is just now spending money with both hands. Hundreds of thousands 'of pounds are being swallowed up in sorely-needed public improvements of almost all kinds, and the town and its inhabitants in the next generation will be much the better for it. To take thought of to-morrow is wise policy for individuals and corporations alike, but the danger of forgetting to-day must not be overlooked. Quite true also is it that a policy of procrastination—as Coencillor Greening says—has been the bane of the town in the past. There is a time for all things, however, and the time is certainly not now for spending £20,000 on a new bridge, which, by the way, has been certified by the Board of Trade as adequate for tramlines. We should all like a new bridge, but positively we cannot afford it yet. Councillor Greening thinks we can,but in alluding to the cost of the deputation to the "Clio " an infinitesimal item by comparison—he says "the rates of the Borough are high, and the Corporation ought therefore to be economical." Need we argue further when one of the principal supporters of the scheme thus controverts himself ?</p>
