      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Brentry Inebriates' Reformatory is known far and wide to these whofeel an interest in the well- being and amendment of the habitual drunkard. At a meeting of the Gloucestershire County Council this week some strong comments were passed upon the utility of the Reformatory " as at present con- stituted." Many will regret to hear that the insti- tution was reported at this meeting to be losing at the rate of £1,600 a year,and that in all probability the loss will continue as long as the men's quarters remain practically unoccupied. A -recent outbreak of insubordination at the Reformatory bad forced events to a crisis,end it became clear to the respon- sible authorities that violent male cases could not be dealt with. The institution had, according to the report of Major General Sampson Way, been a failure so far as the number of reclamations of what may be called "docile" male inebrives is concerned There appears to be a str,mu desire to devote the institution to female patients only, so taking full advantage of all the house room, and the services of an expensive staff. The point involved is one of considerable importance to the whole country, because the Brentry Inebriates Reformatory Was originally regirded as a national experiment. The devotion of the Brentry Rome to tamale patients ' also raises the question as to how the male habitual [ drunkard of very explosive temperament is to be dealt with. Does the exp.rience at Brentry mean that be is beyond the influence of the usual reclamatory methods ?</p>
        <p>The question whether policemen should be pro- vided with bicycles—particularly they of the coun'y—has been much discussed both by the authorities and in the press. In Worcestershire it has been decided in the affirmative, and in a few weeks' time the men in blue will there be careering alang thelroads and lanes of the county,transacting business with far greater ease and far less expense it is believed,than if they were dependent upon the railways, or their own pedestrian powers. But the provision of cycles for the police has yet another aspect, and one that intimately concerns the majority of wheelmen, who to some extent may be said to have been hitherto regarded with disfavour 'by the myrmidons of the law. It has become a matter of notoriety in some districts that the police have peculiar ideas as t ) the rates at which persons summoned for " furious "riding, hays been actually going; and, that a practical know- ledge of the art of cycling and some experience of the true pace that can be made on the highway, may lead the police to take more accurate and intelligent views, is one of the advan- tages which the authorities may reasonably hope and expect to accrue from the use of the cycle in this direction. None of us can be thought to look with a friendly eye upon the "scorching fiend," but that cyclists have had to suffer for official ignorance on this point goes without saying. It is too well known, and sll that sufferers can now do is to hope for the establishment of a better feeling as the result of the experiments about to be made.</p>
        <p>In the event of the grand jury at Bristol Assize having found a true bill in the prosecution arising out of the notorious Al!port affair, the case would commence yesterday (Friday). The editor and re- porter of the " Weekly Dispatch" are represented by Mr J A. Foote, KC, and Dr Evans Austin. For the prosecution, on behalf of the Treasury, are the Solicitor-General, Sir Ed. Carson, K C, M P, Mr H F Duke, K C, M P, Mr Charles Mathews and Mr Sutton.</p>
        <p>Mr Walter Long made an important statement regarding motor-cars the other evening in the House of CJnamons, to the effect that it was under con- sideration by the Local Government Board to compel drivers to take out certificates of competency. Sir IL Vincent, M P for Exeter, has been fined £5 and costs for baying driven a motor-car faster than the law authorised,the defence being that fora number of miles the speed was not more than twelve miles per hour on the average, and it was admitted that the car was driven skilfully. The properei regula- tion, would, of course, apply to the amateur motorist as well as to the professional, and would amount to this, that every person driving a motor- car would have to pass some sort of examination as to efficiency. The motor, assuredly, made a some- what ugly" exhibition "of itself in the coune of the Paris-to-Berlin race, and there have been 'some serious accidents in this country since. The motor-car industry, it will be remembered, was discouraged at the very outset of its career, and it remains to b seen what effect the proposed licence may have. Nor will the feelings of those persons be the most comforting, who have hitherto cherished a belief that certificates would never be required For cycling. That eyelike should again begin to take alarm would ast uaish nobody who recalls the newspaper correspondence of several years ago on the subject.</p>
        <p>The Hon Dr. Johnston, of the Jamaican Legisla- tive Assembly, whose visit to the English provinces terminated at Glasgow on Saturday, has rendered valuable service in making the people of this country familiar with the resources of Jamaica, which country has recently been brought into touch with us through the Imperial Direct West India Mail S3rvice, and also in advancing the claims of the island as a health resort. Dr. Johnston has recently visited Liverpool, Manchester, Glasgow and Elinburgh, as well as Bristol,where a great exhibition of Jamaican products was held, Bath, Cheltenham, and other places, and delivered highly interesting and imnor- tent lectures on Jamaica at each place. Before leaving, he said his visit to England had afforded him the greatest gratification. He was pleaeerl at the enthusiasm which had been created in the eff rrt to revive the prosperity of Jamaica, and mAntioned the circumstance of his having an audience of 4,000 persons at the Giasgow Exhibition to listen to his address. He attributes the fact of his being yet alive to his having gone to Jamaica, and there got rid of consumption. Jamaica lie believes to be the finest climate in the world for consumptives.</p>
        <p>A short time since the Northumblsrland miners by an overwhelming majority declared against the Bight Hours Bill, and now Mr Burt, M.P , in his , monthly report, says the Bill is doomed for the present session. another proof of the almo;t im- possibility of a private member carrying through Parliament any measure that is contentious. Mr Burt becomes sarcastic in referring to the Chan- cellor of the ItYchequer and his coal duty, alleging that, by exemptions, Sir Michael Hicks-Beach sacrifices one-third to one-half of his contemplated income from this source, and asks if it was worth while for so little to disturb a great industry. Some people are still under the impression that it was the coal-owners who made "much age about "next to nothing," seeing that it was not a duty upon coal but upon its export that was at stake.</p>
        <p>-- Returns bearing upon British commerce for the first six months of the new century will not afford par- ticularly comfortable reading for the public gener- ally. Imports certainly show progression, but the increasA is largely due to articles of food and drink received, while in regard to raw materials for manufacturers several articles indicate substantial reductions, the only noteworthy exceptions being those of textile materials. The decline on the ex- port side will not occasion any surprise to those who have followed the returns from month to month.</p>
        <p>"Herapath's Railway Journal" makes note of the somewhat disquieting fact that for the first time in eight years a decrease is recorded in the gross receipts of the principal English and Irish railway companies in the June half of the year. "It is not much," says the " Journal," " only 013 per cent. for twenty-two of the principal lines, bat it is signifi- cant." Until now there has been no falling off in the June half-year's traffic since 1893, when the decrease was 6 3 per cent. All the June half-years from 1894 to 1900. inclusive, have shown increases, two of them (1896 and 1899) of more than five per cent. From this the writer infers that we have seen the crest of the wave, in the shape of a pro- longed trade boom, the last years of which he believes to be fairly in view. " Herewith's" re- marks, in another part of the paper, that it is high time the shareholdsrs in the companies competing for the Scotch traffic should take joint action to put an end to the railway racing to the North. Of course, we have had this kind of statement before, but it may be new to some of the shareholders, and interesting to have it on the authority of "Hera- path," to be told that "these fast expresses do not pay, and are run at the expense of other traffic," and that "we do not believe they are safe"</p>
        <p>WHICH. IS THE :BEST CYCLE? WHY</p>
        <p>THE RALEIGH OF COURSE t</p>
        <p>FROM</p>
        <p>W. H. BELLIAN, BII4.KERIOFITHR IROYALOCAMBELI AN,</p>
        <p>115 ISANICKY-STREET,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JULY 12 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RUNCORN SEs3roxs.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SEs3roxs.</p>
        <p>Messrs J Ockleston anti E lmison presided at the Runcorn Sessions on Monday. and dealt with two prisoners for drunkenness.—They also issued the precept for the Annual Licensing Sessions to be held on the 26th August next.</p>
        <p>DESTROYING THE APPEARANCE OF WIDNES PARK. At Widnes Patty Sessions on Thursday three youths, Richard Ireland, James Hollins, and Robert Whitfield, were charged with unlawfully damag- ing a tree in Widnes Park, with a pistol, on May 24th. Police-constable Turner saw the pistol fired several times, and, upon exlmining the tree, found one branch hanging. The defendants had been fined by the Inland Revenue for not having a licence. The Chairman said it was a very stupid thing to do, spoiling the trees in the park, which were there for the benefit of the public. He would fine them 5i and costs each, and Is damage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNCORN' BOATING CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN' BOATING CLUB.</p>
        <p>Thirty-five members of the alo-gve-iii-un turned out for a picnic to Acton Bridge, on Siturday afternon, when seven of the largest and most comfortable boats of the club, were manned, and taken in tow by Mr F Abel's steam yacht "Winefred," The ran was made from Rock Bavaze at about 3 o'clock,and steaming at about five miles an hour the company reached Acton Bridge, about 430 p.m. Tea was , partaken of in the orchard of the Leigh Arms Hotel, after which, it being Fete day at the village a visit was paid to the fair ground. Football, bathing and other sports were indulged in, and the return journey was commenced about 830 in the best of spirits. Tow ropes were cast off at Dutt3n Lock and the different crews vied with each other in a vigorous row home. The whole pro- ceedings were decidedly enjoyable, and none the least so was the walk home from the boat shed in the moonlight. BOY DaOWNED AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>An inquiry instituted by Mr Coroner Dobson,into the circumstances attending the death of Ernest Shone, six year old son of At Israel Shone, Dover, strew.. Runcorn, showed the deceased, went on Saturday, contrary to the expressed wish of his father, to catch "jack-sharps " in the Old Dock Runcorn.—Alexandra Hughes 45, Bold-street, a deck hand in the employ of the M.,1.0 Co., Thomas Wrench, postman, Dover-str6et ; and two boys Leonard Stubbs,and Leonard Roger- son, all testified to seeing !the boy near the water's edge from about 2.30 to 4 o'clock. Hearing sub- sequently, that the boy was missing, Hughes, went to the spot and grappled for the body which be recovered shortly after midnight,—Constable John •McLeod, who was present when the body was recovered testified to there being no marks of violence thereon.—A verdict of" found drowned" was returned.—Captain Heasley, traffic: superintendent of the M.S.C. Co., who was present, testified to the genuine sorrow the Company fait at such sad occurances, and commented on the use- lessness of fencing such places off No fence would keep boys from going "Jack sharping." (Laugh- ter) There might be the possibility of the Com- pany filling up the Dock, but they did not want to establish a precedent or they might go on filling such places indefinitely.—•;ubsequently ir W 13i/- field appealed to the jury for a subscription towards a set of town grapples, which was fairly well re- sponded to•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOATED.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOATED.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Board was held on Thursday evening last week Mr E Imison presided, and there were also present :—Messrs J Littler, W Handley, J W Lightburn (clerk). and A Lightburn (assistant clerk).—Mr Handley said a man had been to him complaining that his boy could not gain admittance to school: The boy was taken in at the Board Schools for a day or two; and he was then sort home, being told that they had no room for him. He bad been to the National Schools and to Granville-street School, and they said they could not take him in there unless they got a note from the master of the Victoria-road Board School. The parent did not know what to do. He told him (Mr Handley) that Mr Bowyer had warned him that if he did not get the boy in school within 14 days he would be summoned. He (Y.tr Handley) wa4 told he was one of a number who had been sent to Victoria-road School and refused admisqon In reply to the Clerk, Mr Handley said the boy's name was Sydney Sefton, and he was the son of Edwin SMton —This only 'pointed to the fact that they would have to divide the yard at the Granville- street Sichool. The Clerk.—They aro teaching them there. I don't think he has been refused admission at Granville-street.—Mr Handley: He is one amongst others.—The Clerk : Mr Bowyer has told her (the boy's mother) to try Granville- street no later than to-day.—The Chairman : It may be a question for the School Manage- ment or School Attendance Committee to consider.—Hr Handley: He does not want to be summoned.—The Clerk: It is not likely the man would like to be summoned under the circum- stances. He will not be 6ummoned.—lt was deci- ded that the matter should be considered by the School Management Committee.—The returns for the month ending June 28th showed that the per- centage of attendance was 89, as compared with 87 for the corresponding period of last year,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.] The hot weather is entirely responsible for the suggestions below: Could not something `‘e done to provide Runcorn with drinking fountains, and horse and dog troughs at suitable intervals without the rates being in any way r lised. At present there is not a single place, except the Cemetery, where either man or beast can relresh themselves.</p>
        <p>The heat wave is very distressing, and from several quarters of the town cases of suffering therefrom are reported. One or two are undoubt- edly serious, and there is a deal in the evidence given at the inquest on Wednesday to indicate that the tragedy was mainly due to the excessive heat.Dr McDougall also C 013131 nted on the desirability of the dissemination of ambulance knowledge.</p>
        <p>-- The filling-up of old disused Docks, was a ques- tion, also raised at a recent inquest. Captain Heasley of the Manchester Ship Canal Co, mentioned the subject as a possible means of preventing people being drowned therein. Indeed, he even went futther, and said "fencing-off was practically useless."—Personally, I think the filling-op idea a good one. The law ought to step in and demand in the interests of humanity that such " death traps "—for such they inevitably turn out to be—as old docks should be filled in, or other wise rendered inaccessible to the public. Too many lives are lost annually to trifle with the matter and I seriously advocate the District Council taking the matter up on behalf of and as guardians of the pub- lic life of Runcorn.</p>
        <p>--- The secretary of the Runcorn Technical Institute informs me that the local scholarships, held by students at the Institute Day School, are now lensing. and that efforts have been made to raise a fresh fund for this commendable object. The scholarships are of the value of .£4 7s 61 each. The following promises of scholarships and contribu- tions have been received :—Sir John T Brunner, will give six scholarships for one year to en- able students to remain fn. another year for the purpose of taking the advanced stage of instruc- tion. Mr S Taylor, J P, gives £lO a year for two years. The Runcorn Co operative Society, three scholarships for two years. The Highfield Tanning Company, two scholarships for two years. The following pentlemen have promised a guinea a year for two years towards the scholarship fund P Beck, Mr G Bellamy, Mr J Burton. Mr J R Frith. Mr J M Frith, Mr W Handley, Mr E Imison, J P. Mr F 3 Norman, J P. Mr AR Norman, J P, Mr J Pritchard, J PArr w Rowland, Dr Raschen, Messrs Simpson, Davies and Sms, Mr j H S alkeld, Rev A M Wood. Anyone de4rotts of further aiding the scholarships fund should communicate with Mr B Marshall, the secretary, at once</p>
        <p>CRAW FORD j-SI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>CREAM CRACKERS, CRISPAND CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NURSE FUND.</p>
        <p>As the result of the garden party held at Crofton Lodge, last week, Mr and Mrs IL Abel have handed £33 53t0 the above fund. RUNCORN ANGLERS' ASSOCIATION</p>
        <p>In connection with this association a match was held in the Bridgewater Canal on Saturday after- noon. There was a fair number of competitors. under the captaincy of Mr T Worrall. The prize winners were :-1, T Hartland ; 2, P Worrall ; 3, T Baxter ; 4, W Hayes. Sr. MARY'S SIDIDAY SCHOOLS, WIDNES</p>
        <p>On Ssturday the scholars attending the stove Sunday school spent a very enjoyable time at Pen- keth, the occasion being the annual field day. The party left by boat about two o'clock sailing up the St Helens and Widnes Canal. On arriving at their destination they wended their way to a field kind- ly placed at the disposal of the children. Tea was provided by the teachers and everything passed off very satisfactorily. The vicar (Rev C F Stocks) superintended the arrangements. RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The choir sermons were preached at the above Church, the preacher in the morning being the Rev Canon Wood,and in the evening the Rev H B Blogg (vicar of Frodsham). The choir sang Ebden's Magnificat and Nunc Dimittis with marked effect. A special anthem," The Wilderness," by Goss, was sung, the solos being well interpreted by Messrs Branch and J Clare, while the trios and quartettes were admirably sustained by Messrs Dudley, Hewitt, Smith, and Lane, and Masters Wilkinson and Hopwood. After the service an organ recital was given by the organist, Mr F R Boraston, to whom high credit is due for the manner in which the choir has been trained. He submitted the following programme :—" The March of the Magi," "Sunset Melody," "Andante in F," and "The Better Land." The offertories were slightly in excess of last year. LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>The Parish Church, Runcorn, was the scsne on Grounds daughter of Wednesday afternoon of the soLmanizition of the amnadr r iAaigi se 8 °LSi lir G Constance Gj ert so n G, of West Hartlepool, Mrs Grounds, Dock Hot-M, Weston Point. The ian minister was the Rev DS Bowen,Weston Point. The bridesmaids were Misses Hilda and Rose Clark (nieces of the bride), and Miss Gjertson (siiter of The bridegroom). Mr J 11 Mitchinson, of West- Hartlepool, antel. as best man. Mr W Grounds pre- sided at the organ, and was responsible for the musical portion of th 9 service.—Tae honeymoon will be spent in Llandudno.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>INTBRESTING FISHING MATCH.</head>
        <p>INTBRESTING FISHING MATCH.</p>
        <p>The members of both Widnes and Runcorn Anglers' Associatione,spent a pleasant two hour., in a fishing contest, at Keeper's Bridge, on the Bridge- Widnes, was captain, and when he gave " mg oe m" b earbs.o u ts p 03rOt pl i rnoevse were good cast water Canal, on Wednesday night. Mr Hudson, bthye assignmalantyo a capital contest ended (1) S Bailey, (2) A Rawlin-1 son, (3) E Evans, (4) J Bate. All the prize-winners being members of the Runcorn Club. SiIoCKING FATALITY TO A RUNCORN MAN IN AMERICA. News has just been received from America of the death from burns, of Mr Joseph W Clark, formerly of Runcorn. Mr Clark held the position of master mechanic with the Alkali Company at Pittsburg. Pennsylvania. In company with a Mr Edward Archer, he was below ground trying to read the figures on a water meter, and finding there was not sufficient light to enable him to do this he struck a I match, with the result that the sewer was ignited. Mr Clark was so badly burnt, haying inhaled con- siderably of the flame, that be died two days later, being unconscious most of fie time. The deceased was interred at the Cemetery at Saltville, where he formerly resided, the last rites being attended by the local Freemasons Lodge, with which Mr Clark was prominently identified. The burial service was conducted from the Episcopal Church by the Rev R E Bogkin, of Abingdon. Deceased is survived by a widow and Oree children. His fellow workman, although badly burnt, was on June 19',h reported to be doing well. The late Mr Clarke was well- known in Runcorn and district, and the sad news of his untimely end came as a great shock to his re- latives and friends, RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS. On Friday evening the If and G (Runcorn) Com- panies of the 2nd THOR., msrched to the Stone Hills, off Halton-road, to engage in attack and defence practice. A defen.ive position was taken on the bills by the cyclists with a small number of the Volunteers, and the remainder formed the attacking party. The defenders were under the command of Sergeant Phoenix, and the assailants were directed by Colour-Sergeants Mort and Har- vey. The defenders, owing to the smallness of their number, were gradually surrounded, but showed good judgment in the choice of positions. Toe judges were Captain Ashton, Captain Timmins and Lieutenant Linaker. On Sunday morning the batta- Ilion held a church parade to Halton Church. There was a rather poor muster under the command of Captain Ashton, and headed by the band, under Bandma=ter Outhwaite. The Volunteers were addressed by the Rev 6 D Wray. Next Saturday the regiment goes to camp at Salisbury Plain for a ' fortnight. Dress :--Full marching order. RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE. A meeting of the above order was held on Satur- day evening in the Greenway-rnad school at which there was a good attendance of officers and mem- bers. Mr. Thomas lieesom presided. Several matters of interest were broeght before the mem- bers, among others being the auditors' report. This was submitted by Messrs. Alfred Nolen and George Powell, and referred with satisfaction to the finan- cial and numerical condition of the society. The cadets numbered 120 and had £lO3 16s in the bank. There had been an increase of five members during the quarter in the adult branch, making a total of 321. During the quarter,34 members had been on the sick list. £4O 8a had been paid for sickness and .£5 for accidents. After the report had been fully dis- cussed it was adopted, on the motion of Messrs Thomas Clarke and Thomas Cooke A pleasing feature about the meeting was that four youths were initiated from the cadets. Short addresses t rs oR n were given by the following officers :—Messrs men Twhboomsa ggrlyhuyrtge,edAltfhreedyoNunorlg to maintain and D Biebrown, their pledge, to be loyal to the cause, and to do all that in them lay to further the interests and to preserve unsullied the reputation of the eons of temperance. An interesting meeting was then brought to a close. WIDNES, RUNCORN AND DISTRICT BIRD BREEDERS'</p>
        <p>A asocranoN. The Widnes and Runcorn Bird Breeders' Associa- tion held their seventh exhibition of canaries and British birds in their nest feather last week at Councillor Law's Commercial Hotal Widnes. The results were extremely satisfactory and the show on the whole was a very fair one, some very pretty specimens being displayed. The following 14 a list of the local prize winners :-Clear yelliw Yorkshire: 1, G Rathbone ; 2, D Orin; 3, J Ssveetman. Clear buff Yorkshire: 1, D Orm ; 2, r Finney; 3, D Orm Ticked or variegated yellow Yorkshire: 1, D Orm; 2, G Rathbone Clear yellow Norwich,plain head: 1. ER Goodwin; 2, J Davies; 3, T Finney. Clear buff Norwich, plain head: 1 E Goodwin; 2,T Finney ; 3, W J Trevorrow. Ticked or unevenly- marked yellow Norwich, plain head: I, S Andrews; 2. J Davies; 3, F Bennett. Ticked buff Norwich. plain head: 1, T Finney; 2, G Rathbone 3. S , Andrews: Crested bred Norwich : I. W Ridge ;2 and 3, R Jenkins. Yellow-green Yorkshire: 1, J Sweetman; 2, J liampson. Foal, green wing or tail 1, J Rawson ;2, J Sweetman. Foal, green to pre- dominate :1, J Sweetman. Any variety not previ- ously mentioned: 1 and 2, W J Trevorrow, 3, S Andrews. Sitting class: 1,21 Hanipson; 2,5 Andrews. Novice, Yorkshire : 1, F Bennett. Norwich, any variety, novice : 1, F Bennett. Linnet: 1,0 Orm; 2, E R Goodwin; 3, W Ridge. Goldfinch : 1, W Trevorrow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>BREACH OF WARRANTY.</head>
        <p>BREACH OF WARRANTY.</p>
        <p>Interesting Claim_ at Warrington.</p>
        <p>What was the Pony Fed on ?--</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday before his Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C. an action was beard in which Christian Bower, of Appleton, sued John William Hewitt, manufacturer and merchant of George. street, Manchester, and proprietor of a Stud farm at Massey Brook, Thelwall, for £3O damages for breach of warranty in respect to a bay gelding. T:iere was a counter-claim by the defendant for £3l 104.—Mr W J Forshaw regresented the plaintiff and Mr G L Welford of Manchester, appeared for the defendant. Mr Forshaw, in opening the case, said the plaintiff was a butcher carrying on business at Appleton, near Warrington, and the defendant W 219 the proprietor of a Stud Farm at Massey Brook, Thelwall. The farm was managed by a groom, named Grover. On April 5 h the plaintiff went to the farm to buy some horses and Grover showed him a bay gelding which was for sale. Plaintiff said he liked the pony and asked Grover if he could warrant it. Grover renFed that he could warrant it sound in every respect. He added that plaintiff could have it examined by a veterinary ' surgeon, and if tne warranty proved to be right plaintiff would have to pay the surgeon's fee,but if it was wrong defendant would pay it. At that time they could not agree upon a price, but on the 30h April plaintiff again went with a Mr Burrows to the farm and bought the pony for £2B. Grover again repeated his statement as to warranty. They took the pony to a stable where it remained for a short time. ;Plaintiff went to the stable to look at the animal and found it to b 3 lame. He immediately sent for Mr Hewitt, veterinary surgeon, who however, could not examine tue pony that night as it was dark. He examined it the following morning and certified that in his opinion it had a spavin on the off hock. Plaintiff returned the pony Co the farm and it passed backwards and forwarda for some time. At last, however, the defendant retained it, and he had put in a counter claim for the keep of the pony to the amount of £3l 10s. This was computed at the rate of 103 per day—a monstrous charge. Plaintiff gave evidence bearing out this statement.</p>
        <p>In cross-7examination he denied that Grover told him that his master always refused t 3 warrant horses, and that when they left his premises defendant's responsi- bility ceased. H. however, admitted that Mr Hewitt told him that he had instructed Grover to that effect. Harry Burrows, Arthur Taylor, John Thomas, and Robert Brooks gave corroborate evidence with regard to the warranty._</p>
        <p>Samuel .1 Hewitt, veterinary surgeon, spoke to examining the pony and finding it suffering from spavin on the off hock With regard to the counter-claims he was of the opinion that a guinea per week would have been quite sufficient to keep the pony. His Honour : Ten shillings per day is monstrous. You might as well &amp;largo £l,OOO a minute. I suppose oats must bo £l.OOO a bushel here. Mr Welford said that plaintiff was told when be brought the horse back that it would be kept there at his own risk and lOg per day would be charged. His Honour: I should have thought lOs a week would be ample. all I can say is that it has absolutely tak,n my breath away. I could not have believed it unless the pony was fed on turtle soup. (Laughter.) For the defence Mr Weliford said his client absolutely denied the a warranty was given. The defendant was a manufacturer and merchant carrying on business in Manchester and he kept the stud farm as a sort of hobby. He :nada it a rule that under no circumstances would he warrant horses, but intending purchasers might have the animals examined by vaterinary surgeons. The moment a:purchaser took that horse from the defendant's pvernses, the defendant's r-sponsibility ceased. Grover had received instructions to that effect,*and he would swear that in the case in question no warranty was given. Evidence was given bearing out this statement:</p>
        <p>flis Honour cam, to the con-clusion that a warranty had been given, and gave judgmmt for the plaintiff for £2B with costs. The counter-elaim Wag diamissed.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire v Yorkshire Match. A special meeting at the executive of the Lancashire Association was held at the Dog and Partridge Hotel, Old Trafford, Manchester, on Tuesday, Alderman , Meakin the (president) nresidiug. The principal busi- -1 nese was the selection of the venue for the home match I with Yorkshire. Three greens were proposed :—Dids- bury, Dog and Partridge (Old Trafford), and Leigh. and ef these the vote went in favour of Didsbury. where the match will take place on Saturday, July 20h. The Lancashire team selected included H Williams (Warring- ton North), J Williamson (N.wton-le-Willows). F Berry (Warrington South). A J Jones (Warrington North). *J Bennett (Neort•m), *W Wood (Warrington N-arth). The names of those players marked with an asterisk are new inclusions.</p>
      </div>
