      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAT.—Before Mr. Hepherct (in the- chair), Councilloi</p>
        <p>Roberts and Mr. Thornton I.7NIIAPPY MAIMED LlFE.—George Thomas Taylor,Turner- Street. was summone Ifo deserting his wife Elizabeth Tay- lor.—Mr. Steel appeared for the complainant and Mr. Browne rePresented the defendant.—After hearing the evidtnce the magistrates granted a separation order with 103 oer week alimony, he husband to hove the custody of the children. A SaD CASE.—Riebard Kelly, Hindley's Row, Burtonwood was summoned for persistent cruelty lo his wife Georgina 'Kelly. 31 Lairs-street —Mr Steel appeared for the com- plainant and stated the facts of the case which revealed a very sad state of affairs. The parties had only been marriei eleven months, and had lived a cat and dog life." Defen- dant had repeatedly thrashed his wife, inflicting black eyes and scratching her face.—Mr Browne represented the oefendant and ultimately It was agreed to adjourn the case for a month to give the parties a chance of coming to terms. _SED/HATION.—James Slasmith. John-street was summoned for neglecting to provide reasonable maintenance for his wife and family—Mr Steel appeared for the complainant and said defendant had given her nothing since May 25th when he gave her 43.—A separation order with 12s per week alimony was granted.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Mr S Wright tin the chair). Alderman _ Heaton, Councillor Smethurst and Mr Thornton. ITRANGE CONDUCT.—AN OLD OFFENDER IN TROUBLE.— do n Platt, described as a musician, was charged that being a person of suspicious character he was found frequenting Iriam-street, Church-street. Fennel street and School Brow on Saturday night with intent to committ a felony.—Police- Sergeant Barnett said he ft,st saw the prisoner in Butter- market-street about a quarter past ten. He knew him to be a convicted thief. He was peetending to be drunk and jostled several people. He went into 'clam-street and spoke to Mr Taylor, a butcher. Mr Taylor turned into his shop and prisoner extended his hand toward&amp; a piece of meat. but Mr Taylor looked back an I prisoner dropped his hand. He then went into Mr. Jones's picture shdp and coming out after 9 short time stopped a Mr. Hudson and held him for a minute or two. He went further along the street and pushed against a lady close to a tobacconist's shop Superintendent Monaghan then came up and witness told him of the pris- oner's conduct. They watched him further. He went isp Church-street and stumbled against a lady keocking her against the wall. He then turns i back into Fennel-street, stopped some young men and afterwards went into the Traveller's Rest pubiic-house. He came out in two or three minutes, and went into the Raven Hotel, leaving by the Dial-street door. He stopped some young men close to the window. His conduct was similar on School Brow, He Was subsequently found sitting on the doorstep of an unoccupied house in Brick-street. He expressed sorrow for his conduct and said be would go straight home if allowed to do so. He was not drunk.— Mr Jones said the prisoner entered his shoo and saluting him in military style, asked "to haVe his picture taken," Witness told him that he did not take pictures but sold them.—Superintendent Monaghan said he had known the prisoner for 20 years. He had been repeatedly convicted of theft. He had never known him to do any work. There were altogether 27 convictions against him. He was a thorough nuisance.—Prisoner said be had been in the Work. house hospital for eight months and was discharged only. three weeks ago. He had some drink on Si .turday night He did not Intend to rob anyone.—The magistrates com- mitted prisoner to gaol for two month* with hard labour. A I/Imeg ni DRIVER MERTS WITH AN ACCIDENT.— Themes Maguire, a cab driver residing in Union-street, was charged with being drunk In charge of a horse arid cab in Liverpool-road on June 211Ith.—P.S. Evans stated that defen- dant was in such a drunken condition that he had no control ever the horse The vehicle was swerving to and fro and ultimately the defendant fell off the box. He sustained a severe scalp wound and his bands and legs were also Injured. —The Chief Constable said the defendant was one of the most persistent drunkards who drove vehicles. He had been convicted eight times of similar offences It was not right that cab proprietors should employ men with such bad reeords.—Fined 10s and costs' WHAT PoLICFMEN HAVP To TUT TIP WlTH,—Gilbert Harris and James McMullen. Bank-street.were charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Bewsey-street. at half past eleven on Saturday night.—P.o Corbett said he saw the defendants and several other youths on the footpath in Morsemarket-street. He ordered them away. They went Into Bewsey-street where the defendants used very bad language. Witness and P.O Mulroy arrested them. In Scotland-road. the other came upon them said interfered with them. Witness was kicked and his lamp broken. Eventually they got the men away and took them to the police station. They were both drnnk, but did not assault either the other dicer or him.—The Chief Constable said It was a Most disgraceful onslaught on the officer. He was very glad to say for the order of the town that such conduct did not often take place. 'the police oftener got assistance than nything eise.—Fined 5s and costs each. PRAYERS AND CoesEs.—Elizabeth Ellen Fleming, a notorious character. Was Charged with having been drunk and disorderly in DialestreM. on PaturdAy night.—Sergeant Barnett said that be and Superintendent Monaghan were taking a prisoner to the police station when Fleming used filthy language towards them. Witness arrested her. At the Police Station. she commenced to pray and then to swear, repeating each alternately at the Top of her voice.— Defendant Oh I prayed and cursed together did I ? must be very clever.—She was lined 5s and costs. NO ElGHT.—Bernardo Stlonfredi, ice cream dealer, 'flare street, was fined Bs M for driving without a light on th- night of the lath of June. _ . .</p>
        <p>_ A TECHNICAL 01,4e.—Alice and Ellen Lea, provision dealers, 33. Tanners-lane were fined 5s for exposing for sale margarine without having a label attached —lnspector Nor- man prosecuted. He said the margarine bore a small labed half an inch square That could not be recognised. The label should be I} inches square. He had previously warned the defendants.</p>
        <p>TYESDAY—Before Aldcrman Sutton (in the chair), Aldermen Shaw Green and Hutchinsor,. Dr Fox and Mr Blrchall. DOMESTIC INTELICITY.—Joseph Blrchall was charged on a warrant with assaulting his wife Bodelia Birehall ore July Stn.—Complainant stated that shortly after six o'clock her husband came in and withont any provosation seised her by the hair of the head and commenced fo kick and thump her He d rine(' la‘r into the street and kicked her in the face, inflicting a black eye.—Mr Crompton appeared on behalf of the defendant who alleged that his wife was drunk and commenced the "row" herself. She threw a sugar basin at him and ultimately fell against a chair initiating the injury to her face. He did not strike her at version of the affair was corrObor- ated by the defendant's son.—The Chief Constable said the woman was undoubte,ly a drunken woman and had served a month's imprisonment for neglect- cog her children. The man had been before the Conrt thirteen times, but not for any serious offences. Inspector Hackett, N.S.P.C.O . was of opinion that it was a ease of" six of one and half.a-dozen of the other."—The Bench tined defendant 10. and granted a separation with 66 per week alimony.</p>
      </div>
