      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>DECAPITATED ON THE RAILWAY AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>DECAPITATED ON THE RAILWAY AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Remarkable Revelations at the Inquest. Singular Domestic Story. A shocking discovery was made on the Cheshire Lines Railway at Warrington on Saturday evening about 8,30, when the body of a man was found by a porter named Edwin White, on the Winwick- street railway, bridge, just outside the station, with the head severed from the body. The head was lying in the four foot way , and the body in the six foot and it appeared as though the neck had rested on the rail and had been severed by a passing train. The body , was conveyed to the mortuary where it was ' subsequently identified as that of Harry Turley Darlington who had lodged for the past four years at 4 Oldfield Grove, Sale. Mr F A Jones, deputy coroner, conducted an in- quiry at the Warrington Police Station on Tuesday into the circumstances. Mr A R Winter, of Liver- pool, repesented the Cheshire Lines Committee, and Mr H W Gallimore (acting stationmaster for Mr Derbyshire) was also present. Henry Cecil Darlington,Locel Government Board district auditor, 302, Great Cheetham-street, Higher Broughton, Manchester, said the deceased was his son and was 34 years of age. He was formerly a elerk with him (witness;,but he had not known him to have any occupation since. He was living in Sale. He last saw him alive in November of last year. Ile identified his body on Sunday morning. The Coroner handed witness a letter addressed to Mrs Smith ,4,olclfield- grove, Sale, and asked whether the writing was deceased's. Witness said it was. The Coroner then read the letter as follows : " Saturday, 6th Jar, 1901. My very dear Mrs. Smith,—l write to yoa, as you have been my very best friend on earth. You have seen the last of me. The last person I spoke to is Mr Griffin, assistant overseer, of Urmston. Please let my father know. His treatment of me I cannot stand, and.! have no wife. God bless you.-11 T. DARIANOTON. Look out in the evening paper." The Coroner said the letter seemed to suggest that witness's treatment of deceased was not right. He asked witness whether there was any ground for such a suggestion? Witness : Quite the contrary. I have had to keep him and his wife and family for the last four or five years. I have refused to see him ,on some oceasions,lowing to his conduct. The Coroner: Have you heard him threaten to take his life Witness: Several times. When be has been in drink it has been a common cry. We got used to it, and ceased to regard it as a serious threat. Drink was at the bottom of it all. That was the reason be had to leave me. The Coroner : Allow me to express my personal regret, as a brother in the profession with you, in the very sad loss you have sustained and the neces- sity for holding this inquest. It is painful to me, and I know it must be more painful to you. Witness thanked the Coroner. He said his son's death came to him as a very great shock. Charles Griffin, assistant overseeer, Urmaton said he had known the deceased for 17 or 18 years. He had heard him threaten to take his life. He gave as a reason that he had nothing to live for, and people seemed to be all against him. Deceased called at his office on Saturday at noon, and stayed a quarter of an hour He was quite cool and col- lected. He asked for the loan of a sheet of note- paper and envelope and witness gave them to him. (fie identified the notepaper and envelope pro- duced and which bad been used for the purpose of writing to Mrs Smith.) On leaving, he said," You are the last to see me that I know of alive, and you will be subecenael at my inquest, I wish you good day." He then went away. Witness called him back and said " Harry, do you understand what you are saying?" He replied " Yes, perfectly.' Witness said he appeared sober and he replied that he was. Witness then said " I attach no weight to what you are saying, as you have repeatedly made use of similar statements." Ile also asked him why he had told him an untruth when last he saw him He then said he was going abroad. He (witness) advised him to see the gentleman he then men- tioned, and pull himself together. Deceased said he would try. He would see if the gentleman would assist him, and turn over a new leaf. He asked witness for a loan of 24 6d. Witness told him he was afraid be wanted it for drink, and conse- quently declined to give it to him. The Coroner : Very wisely too. Witness (continuing) said deceased thanked him and said he had always been a sincere friend to him He added that he had always been a trouble to his father and family. He again thanked witness for his advice, and left him. The Coroner: Do you know he was given to drink? Witness : Yes, unfortunately: He has usually been under the influence of drinte when he has come to see me, but on Saturday he was cool and collected. Sophia Smith, 4, Oldfield-grove, Sale, said the de- ceased lodged with her. He had been with her about three years and six months. He left the house at half-past nine on Saturday morning. He said he was going to the Stretford Library to look at the papers, and that he would be baele for dinner. She received the letter at half-past five in the afternoon. The postmark was " Urmston, 2,15 p.m." She had heard him threaten to take his life many times. Edwin White, porter on the Central Station, said he was on duty at 8.25 on Saturday night and saw the Derby and Liverpool express arrive. He was on the down side, and when the train went out, he was at the rear: He saw a body on the line. The head was in the four foot, and the body in the six foot. The head was almost separate from the body. It was about twenty yards from the platform to- wards Liverpool. The remains were removed to the mortuary. Mr Darlington asked whether deceased might have been crossing the line when the accident occurred. Witness : No, sir. The train was only just start- ing and went very slowly. The Coroner summed up the evidence and char- acterised the case as a very sad one, The jury returned a verdict to the effen that the deceased committed suicide while of unsound mind, and expressed their sympathy with the family in their bereavement. Mr Darlington thanked them for the attention they had given to the matter. He did not think they could possibly have come to any other con- clueion.</p>
        <p>Women students have littte to complain of in Canada In the new honours list of the fam,us McGill University Toronto the second and foorzta places in first-class honours in the fourth year classes were taken by ladies , whilst in mathematics a lady's name was bracketed for first place. A lady stood second in physics, while in modern languages the honours in all the years were carried off largely by women. The Fiume correspondent of a Vienna newspapers states that on the initiative of the English War Office a judicial inquiry has been opened in regard to the deal- ings of certain Hungarian horse dealers, from whom animals were purchased for the use of the British army in South Africa. The correspondent states that frauds involving a sum of 6,000,000 kronen have already been brought 0 light.</p>
