      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A Local Company's Affairs. At the Manchester Chancery Court on Monday, before Vice-Chancellor Hall, the action of Furness v Furness and Company, Limited, was heard. It was a debenture holder's action, in which Mr Maherly appeared for the plaintiff, Josiah Furness. Counsel stated that the usual order was made in December, 1900, directingluquiries to he made by the Registrar and appointing a receiver and manager of the undertaking of the defendant com- Patly, which carried on the business of grocers, pro- vision dealers, and bakers at Haydock-street, Warrington. The total amount of debentures issued was.lBB7 13s 4d, of which there was due to Ithe plaintiff £l4O, and interest at 5 percent. from the 23rd November. 1900. The whole amount realized by the sale of the property in the security was below the amouut due. Mr Eastwood appeared for the company. Ills Honour ordered the taxation of the costs and the division of the residue amongst the debenture holders. Warrington -Trades' Council Mr E Plinstou presided over the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Councif which was held in the Druid's Hall,Sankey- st evening—The secretary anrneoeut, n es announced that the e Warrington branch of the United Leather Workers Association bad decided to become afillated with the Council and two dele- gates from that body. were Present at the meeting. The Warrington section of the Typographical Asso- ciation had also sent a delegate.—Mr B Pierpoint, M.P.,wrote from London acknowledging the receipt of a letter from the Council enclosing a resolution opposing the Government Education Bill, He pro- mised that the same should nave his attention— Mr J Battersby was appointed to represent the , Council at the annual meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation of Trades and Labour Councils which will be held at Manchester on July 2fth.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF A GIANIESS.—The remains of Mrs Tiarriet Peers, the Cheshire giantess, were on Wednesday in- terred at Birkenhead, of which town she was a native. Mrs Peers was 6ft 4in in height, and abnormally stout, weighing 36 atone. She was 39 years of age,and married at seventeen, having two daughters of average siv,. Since she became a widow shc had publicly exhibited herself. Her father and mother were both very big. Harriett could not stand the beat, and died from apoplexy after two days' illness. The coffin was con veyed to the cemetery on a bury.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY. -- Deficit on the Season's' Work. The President (Mr 3 C Parr) presided at the annual meeting ef the Warrington Musical Society held in Trinity schoolroom on Tuesday evening. Amongst those present were : Dr White. Messrs 3 Newcombe, H Bond, D Fansbaw, P C Price, R Whitfield, J Knowles, with W E Atkin and F J Lord (hon socretaries), L W Reynolds (hoe treasurer), and F U Crossley.(conductor). A com— munication from New York was read from Mr Roger C Parr, regretting his inability to attend. REPORT BY MR. ATKIN. PRESENTATION TO MR. CALD WELL. Mr ATKIN read his annual report as follows At our last general meeting (August 2nd, last year), by your kindness and his own, Mr Lord was elected to be my colleague and I hope in future he will undertake to make the annual report. I notice this is the eleventh time I have had to read it, and I feel I deserve now to be an interested listener, and let someone else make the annual speech. For ten seasons (i.e., since the re-con- struction of the Musical Society in 1890) you have patiently borne with me, and I am sure if you will only give him a chance in future he will make a much more interesting statement. For this last season I must try and Le as brief as possible, and at any rate, I trust I shall not make any remarks for which my colleague may feel inclined . to call me out when we finish to-night's business. The committee decided (as you will remember) to abandon the per- formance of the "Messiah" at popular prices, taking this course with a groat deal of regret on purely financial grounds. It was plainly shown by the treasurer last year that for more than ORB season it was a heavy loss. Three subscription concerts only, there- fore. was eiven as follows : Wednesday, Novem- ber 7th. 1900: "The Golden Legend," preceded by the overture to " Wiliiam Tell." The artistes engaged were :—Soprano, Miss Helen Saxcn ; contralto, Miss Lilian Hevey ; tenor, Mr Lloyd Chandos ; and bass, Mr Dan Price. I believe this was the first time this grand work had been performed in Warrington, and the death of its world famous composer, Sir Arthur Sul ivan attaches a peculiarly melancholy interest to this concert of the Musical Society, occurring as that event did so shortly after that performance. 11. : Wednesday, February 6th. 1901. At this concert an entirely new work by a composer who has lately won for himself much honour in the musical world was given, viz., "The Death of Minnehaba," from scenes from " Hiawatha," by Coleridge Taylor. The vocalists were : Soprano, Miss Lucile Dill, and baritone, Mr Douglas Powell. Mr Carl Fuchs, now, we think, quite a favourite with Warrington audi- ences, was the instrumentalist. His performance of " Saint Sams." " Violoncello Concerto." with the society's orchestra, and his solos with part songs by the chorus, one ot which it will be remembered was by Mr W Johnson, a gentleman very well known in Warring- ton a very few years ago, with the addition of Dr C Hubert Parry's choral ode, " Blest pair of Sirens " (a charming work done by the society on this occasion for the second time), and Schubert's unfinished symphony, first movement, made:up a varied and, we venture to think, most enjoyable programme. 111 : The final sub- scription concert was given on Wednesday, March 20th, 1901. The works included the" Stabat Mater,"" Why Rage the Heathen" (Mendelssohn), for two quartette and two choruses : " Mendelssolen's " " Hear my prayer," and solos, by the vocalists engaged, concluding with the Menet motet, " Oh God, when Thou appearest." We do not intend to do more than just refresh the memories of some who are present to-night by naming each of the three programmes, and shall pass on to the work of rehearsal and a very few other points of interest. Here, however. I cannot help remarking that the concerts just now so roughly revived represents some very bard work; and we do feel a cer - Lain amount of disappointment when we realise that so much good music has undoubtedly failed to attract the more liberal support of Warrington as a whole. It is a most difficult task that your committee undertakes year after year in arranging a prospectus of concerts which shall at once find fitting and proper music for the atten- tion and interest of the members from September to March and at the same time prove attractive to the general public in the form of concerts. We need not remind those present (who are acquainted with the rules of the Society) that its object is not to make money. At the same time we feel sure it is a fact very evident to everyone in Warrington that the concerts are our only means of making the Society's " living wage." We cannot pay our way if we live by the concerts, and we are bound to plead very earnestly indeed for a more liberal support than has been granted us for a few seasons past. I have introduced these few remarks purposely in order that they shall tend to disabuse the minds of many outside the ' working of the society so to speak, for I am convinced that the aim of the musical society is not fully appreciated by a very great many people. Other information was given on a variety of subjests and the report proceeded : During the season we lost a very valuable member of the chorus (Mr Edcles) who for a very great many years has been a most useful and inde- fatigable member of the bass section,and aeso.for along titre a committee man with the interests of the society at heart. He left the town, and I know his regret at severing his connection with the society was fully equalled by the members of the chorus and orchestra and the committee and officers. By Mr Caldwell's resignation the Society has lost an accompanist and organist who for Ike past ten to eleven years has been most faithful in his regular attendance at the rehearsals and concerts, ano we cannot let this opportunity pass without expressing our gratitude to him for his great help. I should like once more to express to all the members (and in particular those who in their official or semi-official capacity are very closely connected with the I routine work of the society,time will not allow me to name them one and all) my hearty and sincere thanks for the support afforded me in my duties. I think no one Can call I the work of the past season anything but a aticcess,frons a musical standpoint,and I only wish our hardly treated Treasurer could follow me in the same satisfactory strain. Financially the result of 1900-1901 season is— well you will soon hear for youreelvee—what it is. I have already touched perhaps more than I ought to have done on this point. I trust I may be forgiven if my keen desire for the success of this long-established society has carried me too far, and I have given expres- sion to Intl feeling too readily, but I am certain I shall be agreed with by all who are interested in the society's welfare, when I say that I honestly think the efforts of the:society are at any rate worthy of more substantjial support than has attended them recently. (Applause.) The beautiful rose bowl, supplied by Messrs Eustance and Co., Sankey-street was exhibited on the table, and , Mr Alerts explained that it was to be presented to Mr. Caldwell, whe was written to asking him to attend, but I he bad replied that be was too shy to do so and begged to be excused. He had, however, thanked the society for their kindness. I The PRESIDENT, said he was extremely sorry 1,2 Caldwell, was too modest to attend, but he hoped Mr 'Atkin would tell him how much his services were appreciated by the society. (Hear, hear.) TINAICCIAL STATEMENT.—AN INCREASED DEFICIT* I Mr L W REYNOLDS (hon treasurer) submitted the inancial statement. Ile wished, he said, he had a more pleasing duty to perform, but it appeared that he would never live to see the society out of debt. Four years ago they had a successful season and reduced their debt freed £95 to £49. Next season they held their own and on the who!n got along nicely. The season before last they lost £3O and this season they had lost over £7O, which was the worst experience be had ever had. He believed that on the first concert they lost £3O, and On each of the last two £2O roughly. The total payments were £340 1.1.8 2d. and the receipts £269 3s sd, leaving a deficiency of £7l Ts 9d. Last year the debt stood at En 14s 81, making a sum total of £154 14i 6d. The PRESIDENT said the reports were not very exhil- arating They were very grateful to both the Secretary and Treasurer for tbe creditable manner in which they had placed the position of the society before them. It was impossible tcr them to put the figures to suit their fancy. They were obliged to give them as they were. I No one he was sure regretted more than they did that the reports were not more satin- I factory. Probably before they separated some I Suggestion would be made in regard to means which I might be taken to arrest the downward course of the society. The last year had been a disastrous one for them as for other societies. That very lamentable event which distressed and disturbed them all, their good Queen's death,and other events bad placed their country almost in mourning, and musical societies particularly had felt the result very keenly and acutely. They might look, however, further back than that and ascertain whether other reasons had not accounted for the losses during not only last year but the year before, and whether the downward course could not be arrested. Mr NEWCOMBE proposed the adoption of the reports. He said the last two years had been disastrous. In the first year the war affected all entertainments, and the Queen's death in the last season, coming as it did at the eve of the second concert, seriously affected both that and the third concert. He thought the chorus and orchestra did not, howevsr, take the same interest in the sale of tickets as they used to do. If they would do the very best they could in that direction it would have a very good effect financially. (Hear, hear). Mr CHARLES WHITE seconded, and it was carried. ELECTION OF PRESIDENT. Mr CHARLES WHITE, in proposing the re-election of Mr Parr as president, said he was the best president they could have. (Hear, hear.) It was a great honour Ito have him occupying such a position. lie was quail- I fled to give an opinion upon music, and he (Mr White) knew that Mr Parr was satisfied with the performances given by the society under the able conductorship of Mr Crossley. Mr FANSHAW seconded. The doctor had said all it was necessary to say about Mr Parr. He believed he (the speaker) was the first to propose Mr Parr as president, and he had felt a great deal of interest in his presidency. The notion was carried unanimously. Mr PARR, in accepting the position, said he felt most grateful to those present for the kind manner in which he had been re-elected. He wished he deserved all the kind things that bad been said about him. One thing</p>
        <p>was certain and that was that he did take a great interest in the society, and he should continue to do so. Possibly it might be owing to some shortcomings on hfs part that had dropped the balance. "N0,n0."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>VICE PRESIDENTS.</head>
        <p>VICE PRESIDENTS.</p>
        <p>Tbe following were appointed vice-presidents t—His Worship the Mayor (Alderman II Roberts) Messrs Z Armitage, John Crostield, J A EJelsten, James Fair- dough, John Fairclough, R T Fairclough, F 'A Frost, R Garnett, Dr Gornall, F Monks, R C Parr, R Pierpointi M.P., Colonel Ridgway, and J White.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>TUE CO3IIIIITTEE</head>
        <p>TUE CO3IIIIITTEE</p>
        <p>D was decided that the following gentlemen should form the committee :—Messrs D Fanshaw, G Johnson, J Knowles, J Newcombe, H Bond,P C Price, W Dotson, R 11 811 cock, W U Wallington and R Whitfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of the PRESIDENT, Messrs W E Atkin and F J Lord were elected hon. secretaries, and each euitatsly returned thanks. The PRESIDENT proposed the re-election of Ble Rey- nolds. and expressed the hope that they would all do ' their best to back him up and relieve him from what was a somewhat arduous position. (Hear, hear.)—Mr CHARLES WHITE seconded, and remarked that after all the debt was not so very great for a Society like this. He suggested the holding of a sale of work. Mr Reynolds was a good creasurer and took a great deal upon himself, and tue work he did reflected great credit upon him. (Hear, hear.)—Tne motion was carried. Mr Resgor.,ns said he was afraid it was beyond his power to bring the Society out of debt unless the public supported it better than they had done. He had done his best with the support accorded to them. (Hear, hear) One thing he did not mention when he read his statement was Oat he had received all the subscriptions, an unusual thing at the time of the annual meeting. In consequence of their large debt he felt his position was a rather onerous one to occupy single—handed. Be thought it would be better if some other member shared the responsibility with him. He would, however, con- tinue to do his test for the Society. (Applause.) Mr G F F White was appointed librarian, Mr Johnson assistant librarian, and Mr Newcombe registrar•</p>
        <p>Mr ATKIN announced that he had received at the meeting a donation of a sovereign towards removing the debt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>TUE PRESIDENT AND TIM CLASS OF CONCERTS _RIVEN</head>
        <p>TUE PRESIDENT AND TIM CLASS OF CONCERTS _RIVEN</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT, in the course Of a few remarks, asked whether they were not emphasizing the educational side of the society and neglecting the amusement part. If they were to depend entirely upon the proceeds of the concert some suggestion in the direction he had indica- ted might be made. As an educational society they were doing good work. They could not possibly do better. Were they to continue that, and work the society at a loss ? As a practical man he was obliged to look at that side of the question. The matters alluded to by Mr Newcombe bad adversely affected the society to some extent, and, again, there were now very many concerts, and other amusements in Warrington. By giving high class amusement possibly they were catering for only a practically small section of the public, though he felt sure the best section ; yet perhaps it was not the best paying section. They might try and cater for all sec- tions. Mr F H CROSSLEY, in referring to the President's remarks, said the question had been asked whether the failure with regard to their financial position was due to the selection of the music, or otherwise. He did not think it altogether explained it; it might to some extent He thought a society to be of use should be considered from two points—the interest and enjoyment of the members and the interest of the public. They knew from experience what sort of a concert would fill Parr Hall. They eould do it if the ladies, or at any rate, the gentlemen would consent to black their faces and do a few negro sketches with a little of the banjo and guitar as an accompaniment. (Laughter.) That was what attracted a Warrhagton audience unfortunately. That was going to the other extreme and it had been sug- gested that a middle course might be taken. It had been suggested they should give one of Gilbert and Sullivan's operas. That was the middle course. It was not very high class music but it was very popular, although in that form it would not be suitable for the society, and he should not care to undertake the work. He would not mind doing one of the works in operatic form. He did not think any light music would attract the members, and the first consideration was the members ; it was for them the society existed after all. This was a matter that required serious thought, and he should be happy to receive suggestions from outsiders and others. They could communicate with Mr Atkin and himself. They had a meeting the other night to discuss the music for next season, but nothing was absolutel3 settled upon. It had been decided by the committee to have three concerts next season. Amongst the suggestions made of the pieces to be per- formed, " The Ancient Mariner,"" The Redemption," and "The Messiah" had been mentioned.</p>
        <p>A discussion ensued and it was suggested that the "Messiah" might be given without outside assistance, that a picnic could be organised, and that a dance might be given. Mr CHARLES WHITE : If we cannot give good reusie let us give up the society altogether • With some can vassing we might obtain more subscriptions. There is a good deal to be done by canvassing. If we put our shoulders to the wheel I don't think £l5O is a great , deal to raise.</p>
        <p>_ The PRESIDENT: It would be better if we coald hit</p>
        <p>the happy medium with regard to the music. Oae or two of the performances have been very beautiful, but a little bit above ths heads of the ordinary concert- goer. Votes of thanks,to Mr Crossley for his services and to Mr Parr for presiding, concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket. Break the news gently! after a disappointing series of defeats and draws, Warrington have at last brought off their second victory of the season. Now was their achievement one of those " getting through by the skin of your teeth "affairs, for on Saturday they trounced Rusholme to the extent of no less than 154 runs. It must indeed be glorious to taste the sweets of victory after waiting for the opportunity so long. It is to be hoped that matters have now taken a brighter turn and that I shall have the pleasuro oaf recording many more handsome victories before the time comes for the sum- mer sport to make its exit.</p>
        <p>It must be mentioned in justice to the Rusholme contingent that they played a man abort. Going to the wicket first they had all retired for the exceptionally small total of 49. R W Kentfield the old Lancashire and Sussex county player put together 26, his strokes including five fours. The rest of the team made a wretched exhibition, not one batsman placing double figures to his credit. Gibson had quite a grand time of it with the ball, and accom- plished the " hat trick." Six wickets fell at a cost of only four runs per wick t, and Gibson must be con gra- tulated upon his performance. E Connor also acquitted himself well, taking three wickets for 26.</p>
        <p>Warringten had not much difficulty in amassing 203 of which J W Bibby was responsible for 59. The War- rington captain was in rattling form and his shots embraced ten fours. Saco.* also gsve an excellent account of himself, his innings realizing 53, whilst E A Connor knocked up an acceptable 22.</p>
        <p>The result of the second eleven's encounter with Rusholme was also highly satisfactory. Getting all their opponents out for 50,they compiled the substantial aggregate of 318. E Pierpoint gave a splendid display and succeeded in augmenting the score by 91 runs. Being anxious to complete a "century "be became too venturesome and was run out. P B Hawti n and T Boughton also did first rate work,eacb being responsible for 56.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Foot Racing.</head>
        <p>Foot Racing.</p>
        <p>The Liverpool Police Athletic Sports were held on the Everton Football ground on Saturday when there was one competitor from Warrington, namly, P.C. J Robin- son, who came in second in the half-mile (Pc•ole and Waring Challenge Shield). He, howeversonly succeeded in getting the third place in the 440 yards race for the same shield. At the first annual athletic sports, pro- moted by the Sheffield Police Athletic Club, on Monday, P.C.Whittaker,a member of the Warrington Police Force succeeded in carrying, off :he fist prize in the quarter mile handicap, open to all police forces in the United Kingdun. Whittaker bad an allowance of ten yard, but he gained first honours somewhat easily from what was the virtual scratch position. His success has gives the greatest satisfaction to his comrades.</p>
        <p>The Police Court and Prison Gate Mission.</p>
        <p>The operations for the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission for Liverpool, Bootle, Warrington Wigan, St Helens, Southport and the Petty Sessional Courts for the month of June show that the missionaries paid 237 visits to the courts and when there conversed with 1,012 persons, of whom 608 were subsequently visited in their abodes. (30 persons were spoken to in the courts at the request, of the magistrates, and 55 others on the suggestion of the police, and court officials. 43 women and girls, and 33 lads were taken to homes, restored to friends, or provided with work. 32 tickets for work were given to men for employ- ment at the Society's works at Bootle, and seven others were found occupation elsewhere. The missionaries in their efforts to help their cases wrote 248 letters. In 26 instances the ministers of religion were asked to take an interest. 88 persons were spoken to in lodging houses. At the Walton Prison Gate Mission Room 616 persons were pro- vided with breakfast and otherwise helped to em- ployment, &amp;c. The number of pledges taken in the work was 176, and 30 reeeting4 were tddressed, &amp;c,</p>
