      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>TO MANXLAND. A Visit with the Warrington Day Tripper.</head>
        <p>TO MANXLAND. A Visit with the Warrington Day Tripper.</p>
        <p>BY " QuiLp N."</p>
        <p>Warrington was, so to speak, deserted last Satur- day week. For the most part the working-cless s pursued the congenial avocation of pleasure seekiag and their sole ambition seemed to be to get es far away as possible from the good old town of wire, soap, and a host or other industries. If only for the brief space of a single day a retreat from the turmoil of a hiaay manufacturing town must be of some benefit to the workingman who leads a some what bum-drum existence in a grimy and un- attractive town like Warrington. Thus it comes to pass that for years the Saturday fol- lowing the great Sunday School Festival, has been recognised as Warrington's "trip day," and in con- nection with several of the leading manufactories in the town, excursions to favourite resorts are organized. The day tripper is a curious individual. Shortly after day break, he sallies forth to the Railway Station, where he meets a multitude of his fellows like • himself, on pleasure bent. He is always careful on this occasion to observe rigid punctu- ality, in fact, he very often arrives at the station half an hoar before the train which is to convey him to" fresh fields and pastures new " steams in. Having reached some favourite resort, he generally makes a practice of visiting as many places of entertainment as he can possibly see with the time and means at his disposal—indulging in a ride on the electric trams, and of ten hiring a boat and for the time being becoming quite an enthusiastic disciple of Izaak Walton. He arrives home an hour or so after midnight, tired, but with the conscious- ness that the day has been well spent, and with many pleasant reminiscences of the'visit to recount. Dougla was invaded by quite a small army of Warrington trippers. They captured the place by storm, occupied it for the day,and then evacuated. To arrange a day trip to the Isle of Man is no small undertsking,yet it has been successfully carried out by the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Company, Limited, for two years in succession. It was my pleasure and privilege to accompany their excursion, and from experience I can say that the outing passed off without any untoward incident to detract from the enjoyment of the trippers. To visit t'ee Isle of Man in a day, of course necessitates an unusually early start from War- rington, and tke three special trains which had been chartered to convey the excursionists to Liverpool, left the Central Station between a quarter and half past four a.m. . Before four o'clock I was on my way to the station and in several streets I came across trippers who had apparently taken upon themselve.s the responsibility of " knocking up " their friends. They were standing in front of the houses gazing intently at the bed- room windows and whistling and shouting at the top of their voices. He, indeed, would be a sound sleeper, who slumbered on under these cireumstances.On reaching the Central Station I found the platform filled from end to end with a seething mass of humanity. Some of them wore blinking like owls, whilst others were very much wide-awake and whiled away the time of waiting by cracking jokes and recounting their experiences of previous trips. By arrangement I met three brother scribes and when the first train steamed in we all managed to obtain a seat in the same compartment. Two of the aforesaid seri oes are enthuisastic devotees of the camera, and one, bent on having some views to remind him of the outing, had brought a camera with him. On the way to Liverpool these two would persist in discus;- sing the merits of a compound known as " hypo ' and also holding forth upon time exposures, quarter-plates. landscape lenses, and a host of other things connected with the photo- graphic art. Myself and the other) scribe knew about as much of photography as of the technicalities of a maxim gun and we were obliged to threaten to forcibly . put . a stop to the eloquence of our fellow pen-drivers if they did not change the subject. After a splendid run we reached Liverpool and the other trains soon followed in our wake. An unusual calm hovered over the streets of the great city, the trams not even having commenced running. The trippers lost no time in making their way to the Prince's Landing stage, at the far n d of which the " King @try " gaily bedecked with flags and adorned with a num- ber of Mes.rs Richmond's "Kruger posters" could be discerned. The excursionists at once boarded the vessel,and proceeded to comfortably ensconce themselves upon deck-chairs. At length thel gangway was drawn up, the ropes cast away, and the" King Orry " left the stage and beaded her bows for Douglas. Warrington trippers must have at last found favour in the sight of the clerk of the weather tor on Saturday the meteorological conditioqs were ideal- Hardly bad the vessel started on her outward course when Old Sol commenced to shed forth his radiance and for the rest of the day the sky remained unsullied. The sea was almost as calm the proverbial mill-pond, and for the greater partNof the voyage there was only a slight rolling motion to be detected. Soon, however, the journey became monotonous, and the: trippers were obviously at a loss to know how to pass the time away. They indulged in walks all over the vessel, peered round the engine room where they inhaled those de- lightful odours which only can emanate from engine rooms, sane ocornic songs, and played con- certinas. Some of them retired to the saloon where they endeavoured (sometimes with success) to get a quiet nap. When nearly four hours had gone by, the rolling became mare pronounced,and many of the passengers were in the throes of mal-de-mer. It was then that I found there was much truth in the saying that " the Englishman takes his plea- sures sadly." One of my fellow scribes who had been chatting merrily ever since we set sail, suddenly became exceedingly quiet, and without any warning, rushed to the side of the boat. He remained in silent contemplation of the mighty deep for about ten minutes, and apparent- ly it did him good. We did not sight the Island until we were quite close upon it, and then every- one's spirits rose. By end by the fine coastline of Mona's Isle backed by richly wooded heights came fully into view, and the scene presented was one that will not readily be forgotten by the excur- sionists. I hold the opinion that the view of Douglas from the sea is one of the most delightful features of a visit to the Isle of Man, and I shall no doubt be supported in this opinion by all who have journeyed to " the little ' Man Island." Mr Richmond and a large number of Warrington friends had gathered on the pier head to bid the trippers welcome, and as the "King Orry " came alongside the jetty a hearty cheer was raised' All were glad to see the gangway thrown out, and to set foot on terra firma once more. It was a happy idea for Messrs Richmond to pro• vide a substantial breakfast for the excursioniets immediately on arrival To accommodate this large party, four restaurants had been engaged, and the wants of the visitors were most expedi- tiously attended to. After the tedious voyage one can imagine the avidity with which they attacked the abundance of good things provided. Mr Richmond visited each. res- taurant in turn and welcomed the tappers most cordially. After breakfast the excursionists proceeeded to the'Wellington Hall, where a pre-</p>
        <p>" In the organism of a man as that of all the 'highest anim ils, the materials for the nutrition of every portion of the structure are supplied by the Blood. If the blood is deceased the boly is deceased " Clarkes' World famed Blood Mixture" is a guarantee cure for Blood and Skin Diseases. It is the most searching blood cleanser ever din' covered and,"and will free the system from all impurities from whatever cause arising. For Scrofula. Scurvy, Eczema, Bad Legs, Pimples and Sores of all kinds, its effects are mar- vellous. Thousands of testimonials. Sold everywhere at 2s 95 per Bottle. Beware of worthless imitations and sub- stitute.—Advt. FLORILINK r—VOR THE TEETS AND BREATE.-- ThOrOngtly cleanses the teeth from all parasites or impurities, harden the gums, prevints tartar, stops decay. and gives to the teeth a peculiar pearly whiteness and a delightful fragrance to the breath. Price Is. 6d for the liquid, or Is. per jar for the Florillns " Powder," of all Chemists and Perfumers. Adtt VALUABLE 019209E8T FOR THE your hair Is turning grey or white or falling off use the MEXICAN HAIR RENEWER, for it will positively restore, in every case, grey or white hair to its original oulour. It makes the hair charmingly beautiful, as we las promoting the growth. Price 136 65 per bottle.— Ad lt• To MoTergaq.—Mrs. Wins low's Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their hildren while teething, with perfect success. It will relieve the poorsufferer immediately.. It is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural ouiet sleep, oy relieving the child from pain and the little cherub awakes "as Iright as a button." Of ell Chemists Is lid per bottle. Advt. CLARKE'S B 41 PILLS are warranted to core, in either sex, all acquired or constitutional Discharges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the back. Free from Mercury. Established upwards of 30 years. In boxes 4s. Fid. each, of all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors through- out the 