        <p>sentation of sixty guineas and an illuminated ad- dress to Mr II E Ransoms, the retiring works manager, took place. They were now at liberty to spend the remainder of the day as inclina- tion dictated. Bent on making the most of the opportunity afforded of seeing some of the beauties of the Island, I and my three • friends de- cided upon visiting Groudle, famed for its beauti- ful glen and bay. With this object in . view we wended our way to the beach, in order to walk to Derby Castle and there board one of the electric trams. A refreshing breeze was blowing from the sea, and the clear water of the bay was breaking into serf upen the heach, which was strewn with seaweed and pebbles • The short ride on the tram was an enjoyable experience as it enabled one to obtain a comprehensive view of Douglas and its crescent-shaped bay.ln about twenty minutes the car drew up in the picturesque station at Groudle, and we at once directed our steps towards the glen. The sylvan beauty of the glen, with its rustic bridges, rich foliage and flora, and dashing waterfalls excited our unstinted admiration. The babbling stream is as clear as crystal, and in it hundreds of trout abound One part of the glen takes the form of a fine canyon, and Le and there the shamrock and heather grow in rich profusion. My friend with the camera was afforded plenty of scope to pursue his hobby to his heart's content and the frequent click of the spring denoted that plenty of views were being "snapped." At one extremity of the glen, a tree trunk had fallen across the brook, and was resting temptingly - on each bank' Two of my companions were determined to be photographed while seated on s the trunk, but having some qualms as to the safe 4y of the position I decided not to be " taken." My friend the photographer took it into his head that he would like to form one of the group. so after giving me minute instructions as to when and how to press the button, he commenced to mount the trunk. He had almost got into a comfortable position when a snap was heard followed by a splash. The trunk had broken, precipitating the three of them below. Two of the scribes managed to alight near the bank, but the other descended gracefully into the midst of the water. At the same time his straw hat came floating down the stream, in all making a picture well worth reproducing The opportunity was too good to be lost so I pressed the button. The pho- tograph has not yet been developed but it is ex- pected that a pair of not very delicate feet emerg- ing from the foam will form the most prominent feature of the picture. After fully exploring the Glen we returned to Douglas, and having partaken of tea, ascended the Head. From this delightful eminence a splendid panorama of Douglas met our gaze, and it was interesting to watch the steamers sailing to and from the pier, leaving a long trail of foam behind them. It was almost time to commence the return journey when we descended into Douglas, so that after having a last stroll along the pro- menade, we made our way to the "King Orry." Quite a large crowd assembled on the pier to „witness our departure, and as the vessel set sail for Liverpool, they cheered enthusiastically. Gradually the shores of Mona's Isle faded out of sight, and soon we were once more in the open sea. There was an invigora• ting breeze, and the return voyage was most enjoy- able. The trippers were in a merry mood, and the passage was not nearly so monotonous as the outward journey. One of the excur- sionists occupied his time in extracting wierd sounds from some instrument of music (?) whilst one of his friends attempted to dancea jig. After treading on several aender toes he alighted unceremoniously on the deck,and for the time being had had quite sufficient of dancing. The lights of Liverpool soon appeared,and shortly after twelve o'clock the landing stage was reached. The trippers arrived at "the good old town" about 1.30, and there was a concensus of opinion that the ex- cursion had been the most successful ever held in the annals of the firm, Dawn was just beginning to break when I reached home, and as I saw it break the previous, morning I was not sufficiently interested in the process to stay and watch. In- stead! capped an enjoyable outing with well- earned and, sweet repose</p>
      </div>
