      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS:</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS:</p>
        <p>• MOST of us live in a continual state of expecta- tion at somebody doing something. NONE of us want, to hurt anybody else's feel- ings ; but it sometimes seems necessary to our own peaee of mind. Josies Why don't you join our shooting party ? " Smitn " Well, I'm a poor shot, and I'm afraid you w•P in ke ctarrie of toe." t-OaleLlsiaNT Ma:den's inauth, a woman's eyes, a kairg ai.'a ;tod au-old man's figure, and a reLtu' or good !aste and discrimination is yours. UP TO EXPaOTArluNd.—He: "You look sweet enuagla to kiss ii. that. dress." She: "My dress- maker told rue that she didn't think I. should be ppointed in it." .acotesaseat..—" Have you seen Cherie", Lamb Pima; his ace:de:it ?" "No ; is he badly hurt? Waal, ami.,set •:" "He made a couple of thousand pi., • I in the ::::tock Exchange." efasestx es AND OTIIEU.S.:•--"Wil remarkable," said the pelt lical star, " ooW differently people are affected by the same thing." "How do 'you mean?" ".Well, I was thii2Eillg of my. speech. It kept me awake four nights, and put everybody .who heard it to sleep." - WIPE: "The newspaper says that Mr:Ha:filet fiftnittisrs reCeived an ovation. What does tnat mean ?" Husband: " .I:he word ovation,' my dear, is derived from the Latin ova,' whicn means an egg. Ovation means a shower of eggs." A Clostraceutsfhei Excuse. —lnnocent Litt;e Wifie "You needn't hurry to get up, darling. I've just wired to the office to say that you feel so unwell after working so late at the bookS last night that you can't attend business to-day." MORE SOLEMN.—"It is a soletou thing," said the young man, "when a woman trusts a man with her affection." "It ain't as solemn," ..aai.the loan with the yellow necktie, "as when si:c sue% trust him with his own wages." Mrs. Wismes "What most surptised you when you arrived in Paris?" Mrs. Jaunter: "Why the fact that little children of tbie or four years could talk French. It was perfectiy- amaz- ing." " Timorne wouldn't marry that rich widow when he found out she was deaf." "What difference would that make ?" "All the neigh• hours would hear him when he asked her tor money." .</p>
        <p>WIFE: "Mrs. Brown is very much attached to that pug dog, and it's as ugly as sin." Husband: "My dear, if sin were as ugly as Mrs. Browife pug dog the straight and narrow path would 1),. crowded." PRACTICAL FATHER : "If he says he loves you, I supposes he does ; but can he support you?" Daughter: "Why, papa? You must know that it wasn't his fault that the chair broke." DEALER "I am sure, madam, you could look the city through and not find a handsomer car- riage than this." Mrs. D'Avnoo "Oh 1 It's handsome enough, but it looks too comfortable to be stylish." His WIFE: " Why don't you go to the doctor and find out ,ust what you ought to eat and sc hat you ought to avoid?" Dyspeptic: "Oh, I know all that now. I ought to eat everything I don't like and avcid everything I do." CHILD: "Mamma, what is a common person?" Mother: "Why, child, a common person is, is— well, it's a person we do not associate with. Why do you ask ? " Child : "'Cause Mrs. Nextdoor said you was a common person." TOMMY, aged eight, had been sent to a near-by store to purchase a pair of shoe-strings for his mother. "How long do you want them ? " asked the clerk. "I don't want ;o borrow 'em," re- plied Tummy ; " I want to buy 'ern for keeps." " DERE'S always bound to be kickers," exclaimed Meandering Mike. "Did you ever know a time when de people agreed unanimously dat dey had de right man in de right place?" " On'y once," replied Plodding Pete: "I was bein' put into gaol on de occasion." A COUNTRY GENTLEMAN, wishing to say Some- thing polite to Dr. B—, the last translator of Juvenal, addressed him in the following words : "What particularly convinces me of the faithful- ness of your translation is, that in places where I do not understand Juvenal, I likewise do not un- derstand you." LITTLE JAMES had been imparting to the minister the important and cheerful information that his father had got a new set of false teeth. " Indeed, James?" replied the minister, indulgently. "And what will he do with the old set ? " "Oh, I s'pose," replied little James, "they'll cut 'em down, and make me wear 'em." "OUR cat has just had chickens," remarked Toadhole. "Nonsense, Mr. T—," snorted his spouse. "You must be inebriated. I suppose you mean our cat has had kittens." "No, I don't," meekly murmured the poor fellow. "1 brought home a couple of chickens for to-morrow's dinner, but Sarah tells me the cat has eaten them." VANDERBILT, the millionaire railway mangate, wrote very illegibly. Once he dismissed an officer for dishonesty, but the only legible part of the document being the signature, the man used it for years as a free pass on the railroads. OLD MONEYBAGS: "Mr. De Stoole, you must be more punctual. I notice that you are late getting to the office nearly every morning." Mr. De Stools: " Yes ; but, Mr. Moneybags, did you euer notice how punctual I am in going away from the office every night?"</p>
        <p>_ HE: "For my part I can't see why you women should want to ape men." She: "Oh, anything for a change, we've been making a monkey of him long enough," DIDN'T I tell you never to darken my door again 1" cried the irate parent to Tompkins. " Yes, Mr. Hicks, you did; that's why Ive come at night, sir." Your door is so dark then that my coming couldn't possibly make it darker.' MISS BELLE: "Mary, remember I am at home to none except Mr. Vere Brownkins this after- noon." Mary (half an hour later): "I've told four gentlemen callers that you are at home to none except Mr. Vere Brownkins, miss, and they all went away very angry indeed, miss." BENRATII CONTEMPT.—Mr. De Broker: "I am a defaulter, and I want you to defend me." Great Lawyer: "Certainly, Mr. De Broker. I'll get you off all right. Have no fear. How many millions is it?" Mr. De Broker (with dignity) : "Sir, I am short only a few thousands and I hope to pay that some day." Great Lawyer (to office boy) : "James, show this vile scoundrel the door." Do UELY BuorliEßS.—Robert Browning was once introduced to the Chinese Ambassador, the intro- ducer, who acted as interpreter, observing that they were both poets. In the conversation Brown- ing asked how much poetry his excellency had written. "Four volumes,' was the answer. "And what style of poetry does your excellency cultivate?" " Chiefly the enigmatical," replied the other. "We felt doubly brothers after that," was Browning's subsequent comment. SAVED BY tZTEEL.—" Go !" she exclaimed, and never speak to me again I" He passed out into the unsympathetic night, but paused when he reached the sidewalk, and drew something from one of his inside pockets. As he did so the beauti- ful gifil who was watching him from the vestbule uttered a shrill scream and ran towards him. "Albert," she cried, "what would you do? Pray do not kill yourself Throw your revolver away, and let us forget our quarrel." "it isn't a revolver," he replied, as he eau; lit her in his arms. "It's a spanner I borrowed from you that day my wheel broke down. I have been wearing it next to my heart ever since." They then returned to the parlour and took up the thread of love's yonng dream where it hail been broken. "MY dear," whispered the young man, "as we are so soon to be married, we should take a practical view of life and profit by the mistakes of others. For instance, theme is the subject of a regular allowance every week for spending money, you know." "Oh, I've thought of that," she replied, sweetly. " Have you ? ' "Yes, indeed. Hundreds and hundreds of times; and lately I haven't thought of much else." "You know the amount of my income, and that I want it to go as far as possible towards our happine*," "Of course. Well, I've talked it over with mamma, ane she thinks an allowance of one dollar a week will be plenty." "Indeed !" " Oh, yes. You can walk to the office and carry your luncheon, you know, and so you can use the whole dollar for cigars and neckties and thin2s." ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATAIRS.—Advt LADIES BEST GLACE BOCTS •!'D SNOB UNDER MR CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. PUBLIO BENEFIT UNDER THEO CLOCKFoa BES r BOOTS mis LANCA SHIEB.—Advt ASK for'CUNNINGIUM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER THE CLOCK.— UNDER THE CLOCK on GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>BASSINBTTEN AND MAILCANTS in latest Art Shades —J, ii. GAALAID,SI, 53 and 55,Bridgett. Wartington.—Advt</p>
        <p>7Lettero to tbe abitor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one. We cannot under ordinary circumstenCes under- take to return the manuscripts which are not used.</p>
        <p>OPMEI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Right of Free Speech.</head>
        <p>The Right of Free Speech.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt gave el-r- Birch&amp;ll pain to write his slashing titter to you on the Queen's Hall meeting,but it afforded me immense pleasure to read it. I knew there were more anti-war people in Warricgton than a perusal of the local papers would lead a stranger to believe, but they have the disadvantage of not having had a local paper to voice their views on the war. The pleasure I experienced was not so much because you sir, received so severe a slating at the hands of Mr Birchall, though that contributed somewhat to my satisfaction, but because it was an exposure of the methods adopted by the jingoes of calumniating not only the Boers but those who sympathise with them in their struggle against a long designed attempt to force war upon them and annex their country. When just before the war broke cut, I read the daily accounts of the Dreyfus trial, I was filled with amazement at the un- reasoning fury of the French people ; and at the absolute indifference to the moral issues involved. The impression I and everybody else obtained was that the French did not care two pins for justice ; all they cared about was "the honour of the army." Then with that air of British superiority which distinguishes the Englishmen from the general ruck of mankind I admin- istered to myself an approving slap on the chest and eaclaimed that such things could not be in dear old England. I had not long to wait before I had a rude awakening. I have seen my fellow-countrymen trample upon every principle that exalteth a nation, and though I admit that while many who differ from me on the origin of the war do so with perfect sincerity, a very large and noisy mass do not trouble themselves whether we are in the right or wrong. It is sufficient for them that the Boers are at war with us, and must therefore be beaten, if possible exterminated for the "honour of the army." These flatter themselves that they are patriots when they are merely scoundrels. They are the mindless and heartless re outh-warriors, , who advocate the "ducking" of that noble minded Englishwoman, Miss Hothouse, because she tells the story of sorrow and suffering produced by the Weylerian methods adopted by the Government against Boer women and children. Their attempts to break up an anti-war meeting were instigated by the gutter organs of the press, and excused or approved by others, on the ground that the promoters at-ked the government to "sue for peace." This is not a true description of what the promoters did, but if it were it would furnish no justification for this outrage on the right of free speech. Personally I hold that to sue for peace is the only honourable course for England to take. I believe her to be entirely wrong in this war, and the sooner she gets out of it and makes amends the better. The doctrine that the opponents of the war have a right to their opinions, but no right to give expression to them is monstrous. I can imagine a Russian autocrat or a Turkish despot holding a view of this kind, but it wont do for a free country. For anyone to state such a doctrine in England is but to make himself ridiculous. It strikes at the root of representative government. If a citizen believe his government to be taking a wrong course he has a right to say so. Those a approve of the government's action may criticise bin and may even call him names till they are black m the face (in private), but to take any other course they would run the risk of falling either into the arms of the law or against the fist of their opponent—perhaps heti): Every person has the right, nay has impesed upon him the duty to exercise to the fullest his power and influence in shaping the chstinies of his country. In recent years the churches have wakened up to this duty, but thei neglect of it in this instance (with some noble excep tions) has done more harm, in my opinion, than will be undone for a generation. Yours ke, THOMAS EDMONDSON. *</p>
        <p>_ Walden, Fearnhead.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Thanks for your admission of my letter last week, though your printer or someone else made slight alterations which were not improvements. I called the war fratricidal not finaacial as you printed it. I named the Bishop of Hereford as reproaching the Chris- tian Churches for their guilty silence during this terrible criSis.You printed the vague statement "as made by one of the Bishops". You say I leapt before I locked,but I think I your comment is wide of tbe mark. You say you got your information about the Queen's Hall meeting from what you consider the soundest Liberal paper in London. Well, I can only say that any paper hich gave such a garbled and one-sided report, and which charged I some of the best Liberals in the Kingdom with " packing a meeting" and employing " Hooli- gans," is not worthy to be called a Liberal paper. Had this paper anything to say about the circulars dis- tributed for days before,and the paragraphs in the jingo papers inciting the people to violently suppress free speech at that meeting ? And that several thousands bad responded to the call who had to be driven away by the police ? I suspect that these facts were conveniently Ignored by this Liberal paper, or treated as only humor- ous incidents inseparable from the occasion. And it is to this depth of degradation we have fallen, and our boasted right of free speech has made us the laughing stock of Europe. It is papers such as this that made possible the scenes that occurred last week at Stratford-on-Avon.when four Liberal meetings wete vielently wrecked within a week, the authorities mildly looking on. Miss Hobhouse, a lady of high character, who has spent much time and labour amongst the women and children in the concentration camps, and has reported the awful mortality existing which far exceeds the rate of mortality from the plague in India, and who is desirous of influencing the benevolent people of this country to lessen the suffering that exists to such an awful extent—this woman is Tefused a bearing in many of the large cities of this once free England of ours. All the national savings of 50 years are gone not to mention some 30,000 slain and crippled fellow country- men of ours, and we are piling up debt at the rate of at least a million and a half a week, and which must I eventually come out of labour. No wonder peoole are beginning to ask what have we gained by it all ? Yours faithfully, T BIRCHALL. I</p>
        <p>[Mr. Birchall did not name the Bishop of Hereford in his M.S. The name was missing and we altered ,the sentence to make it read intelligibly.—Bd. ",liir.l</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>,Corporation Contracts.</head>
        <p>,Corporation Contracts.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is easy in these days to secure a letter of aynapathy and a testimonial to character. Not an offend- er of any kind, small or great, but is " somebody's darling" I During the past two or three weeks I have been, so to speak, overwhelmed with letters, anony- mous and otherwise, approving of my scion in regard to this question,but I am notgoing to ask you to publish any of them ; it is unnecessary ; you could not afford the requisite space, and I can fight my own battles. I think your milers will agree with me that there is really nothing in the Mayor's letters calling for notice. I will only say that in my judgment there is a debit side as well as a credit side to his sixteen years' connection with the Corporation, and I dcubt if the advantage has been with the latter. .</p>
        <p>Mr WH J Jenkins is plausible enough ! He confesses that he was himself a party to a " searching investiga- tioh "Into the Mayor's position in regard to Corporation contracts, but now says In effect that it is " hitting below the belt" if when a man has been elected to the Mayoral office, all differences political, religious and otherwise are not sunk. Let me ask Mr Jenkins what differences political, religions, or otherwise were sunk at Manchester in November last when Aldermat. Higginbottom, then the Lord Mayor elect, was found to have been trading with his Corporation, though only to a limited extent as compared with what has been going on and is going on at Warrington? The Lord Mayor elect of Manchester was forced to resign his seat humiliated and discredited. IC is true that Alderman Higginbottom was trading as a partner in an unlimited firm which made4bis trading strictly speaking, contrary to law, but as has already been shawl], ii. was the moral delinquency which really brought about his enforced retirement. At the time of the retirement of Alderman Higgin- bottom our own Mayor decided that lienzeforth he and his family would cease to take their share of the profits atising out of the relatively small amount of direct orders received by hie firm from the Corporation (I cannot conceive how this can be done) while continuing to take their share of the profits arising out of the relatively very large indirect orders received from the Corporation through sub-con- tractors. It would interest the ratepayers of War- rington to know why the Mayor distinguishes between the profits of direct or indirectltrading ; indeed why he gives up any profit at all, if as he says, he is in" an nnassailable position both as regards law and morals." , Yours, &amp;c., JAMES DODDS, Warrington, sth July, 1901.</p>
        <p>The War and the Capitalists. Sir,—There is one aspect of the South African war that cannot too often and too seriously occupy the thoughts of the workers of this country ; and that is the promi- nent part played by the capitalist element in its pro- motion, and the attitude of this section towards the labour question. We have heal asked to believe that 1 the millionaires of South Africa. where there are more to the square•mile than in any other quarter of the globe. were so deeply moved by the voteless condition of the gold miners on the Rand, that they were prepared to go any lengths to secure the franchise for them. Such a thing had never been know* before. If these men had been actuated by a sincere and disinterested motive to enfranchise the Outlanders, it would have entitled them to a measure not only of respect but of gratitude, although it might not have justified recourse to arms</p>
        <p>One never knew such perfect unanimity on the part of the wealthy, and particularly of great financial i101.157FS to give votes to men who did not care to have them. In this country we had to threaten destruction before we could even pet a property qualifi- cation. Men who had never been known to vote or speak for an extension of the fran- chise in this country would have given the broadest qualification in the Transvaal. The working men at home were not fit to be trusted with a vote, as it would be a menace to the sacred right of property, but no such scruples embarrassed them when others' property was concerned. Young men in this country have deluded themselves that they were going out to fight for "equal right to all white men," although they hadn't got even a lodger vote at home. Oh the comedy and the tragedy of it !</p>
        <p>Who then at this time believes that we went to war for any such thing P This was a mere pretence that ought not to have deluded a single Englishman for one moment. The war agitation against the Boers was en- gineered by men who sought to improve their own floancml interests. Mr Hays Tlammond, as the head of one great South Afn __a Company wanted cheap labour, and because tl Transvaal laws would not cater to his cupidity he ex I.ts at a shareholders meeting that, thanks to the war, he will be able to ob- tain labour on his own terms. The less you give a native he says, the more he likes it, One of Mr Rhodes great companies set forth in its report that " the government will take care that in some form or other the natives, who have not hitherto been directly taxed, shall now contribute to the expenses of the war and of the government of the country, which will have the effect of making them an industrious and useful portion of the population." What does this mean? It means that they will be so taxed as to compel them to work in the mines a form of labour they detest. Under presi- dent Kruger they were not subjected to this taxation but through the pressure of the mine owners it was made lawful for magistrates to order natives to be dogged to induce them to work. This act has received the sanction of the British Government. Thus by means of the whip, plus taxation the mine owner will be able to obtain cheap forced labour and he will be happy. Mr Albu. one of the fiaaacial clique was asked before an enquire how he would cheapen natlve labour. He replied," By simply telling the boys their wages would Ibe reduced." Questioned further this man said in the event of the natives declining to work he would make labour compulsory by taxing them. This is a matter which wage-earners should ponder over very seriously. We cannot affect to be indifferent to what is paid to workers three thousand miles away. The labour ques- tion has a continuity which compels its consideration as a whole. A blow struck at'labour three thousand miles away is a blow struck at home ; and had the people of this country accurately gauged the designs of the financier-Imperialists we should not now be welter ing in this disgraceful South African mess.—Yours &amp;c., T. EDMONDSON. Fearnhead.</p>
      </div>
