      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTOR. How he was Received at the Warrington Police Court. Deputy-Mayor Leaves the Bench.</head>
        <p>A CONSCIENTIOUS OBJECTOR. How he was Received at the Warrington Police Court. Deputy-Mayor Leaves the Bench.</p>
        <p>William Dalton, of Golborne-street, appeared at the Warrington Police Court, on Wednesday, to make an application for a certificate exempting his child from vaccination. The magistrates on the Bench were Alder- men Fairclough (deputy-mayor, in the chair), Heaton, and F W Monks, and Messrs Birchall and Geddes. The applicaat stated that .'his reason for applying for tbe exemption was that be had already lost one child through vaccination, whilst another had suffered severely. The Chairman: How many deaths have there been in Glasgow?</p>
        <p>Aplolicant: I cannot answer that question. The Chairman: And you would let all the people in Warrington die for the sake of y our child? Applicant did not make any response to this question but said he had a conscientious objection to vaccination.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (rising) : I have a conscientious ob- j.ction to granting such applications, and I am going. (Alderman Fairclough then left the Bench). Alderman Heaton said he had never been vaccinated and never would be. He had seen plenty of butchery without being vaccinated.</p>
        <p>Mr Bi-chall said they must find fault with the law and not with the applicant. He supposed they had to grant the application, if they were satisfied that he had a conscientious objection. me application was then granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>SUNDAY EVENING.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY EVENING.</p>
        <p>Hail blessed eve of rest and peace Whose closing shades draw nigh; How many of those in sweet repose Have thought ot Him on high? How many have wandered far and wide, O'er veldt and dewy mead ? Without a care or thought of Him Who provides for all oar need. How have our sailors spent the rest. What may their thoughts have been f Whilst the setting sun sinks in the West All in its glorious sheen. This sacred eventide of peace, Hew have the poorest spent; Have they partaken of the rest. • Their Blessed Saviour sent. Oh peaceful eve, calm and serene, The brightest one of seven ; God grant that at our last eventide We may live with Him in Heaven, ARTHUR J. THORP. 67, Padgate-road, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
