      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>Reported Big Fight in the Transvaal. Fifty Boers Killed and Many Wounded.</p>
        <p>The " Daily Express" correspondent at Lourenco Marques states that a large commando crossed the line near Belfast, and a big fight took place be- tween Machadodorp and Lydenburg. The Boers (the correspondent adds) were defeated, with heavy loss, at least 50 being killed, while a large number were wounc,e(l.</p>
        <p>Third South Lancash!res Returning.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colotel R J Black burne, Majors M Et Hall and A F Tarbot, Major-Adjutant R H B Smythies, Cap- tains X Enkersley, J Vaugnan , and R A Greg, Lieuten- ants A El Spoiner, H G Clarkson, and R B Neill, Lieutenant-Quartermaster J Morrell, and 659 men of the 3rd RAitla Lancashire Regiment are on passage home by the Pinemore, which left Cap3town on July 6th, and is due at Queenstow n on July 28th and Southampton on July 30:h.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Return of Captain Cary.</head>
        <p>Return of Captain Cary.</p>
        <p>Captain Cary, who went to South Africa in charge of the second contingent of Warrington Volunteers, re• turned to Warrington on Saturday evening, with five men. They came over in the steamer Montford,which, it may be remembered, as stranded near the Isle Gf Wight. Captain Cary arrived at the Central Station at half-past six on Saturday evening. The time of tits arrival was not generally known, and there was, there- fore, but a small attendance on the platform. The captain, on Irhalf of the Warrington battalion, was met by Major Buckton,Alajor Roonie, Captain Appleton and Lieutenant VVillcocka. Major Buckton accompanied the returned soldiers to the barracks, and shortly after- wards they were dismissed, and left for their respective homes. On Sunday night Captain Cary left Warrington for Carlow, Ireland, where he resides. The five men who returned with Captain Carey' are Privates A Horton (Marsh Elouse.lane), El E Riley, Singleton (Earlestown), Singleton and Westerman, (St. Helens Volunteers). Privates !Fitton, Riley and Single- ton left the Infantry company Volunteers on the 4th of November, 19)0, and proceeded to Pretoria with the Mounted Infantry Company, where they were occupied in scouring the country for Boers, and were under nom mand of General French. They went through the &amp;a- -1 tern Transvaal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Honouring the Dead.</head>
        <p>Honouring the Dead.</p>
        <p>Memorial Tablet linveiled at Orford Church. The congregation at Orford Church did honour on Sun- day evening to the memory of the late Private F Woods, a native of the village who went out to South Africa with the first c mtingent of Warrington Volunteers and unfou unately succumbed to enteric fever. The late Private Woods was a regular attender at the Church and was also associated with the Sunday school, so it was felt by many of his old school fellows and others, that his patriotism should be fittingly recog- nised, and subscriptions were solicited so that a suit able memorial might be erect, d. It was decided that it should take the form of a tablet in the church, and at the service on Sunday the memorial was unveiled. The tablet is of white marble and bears the following in scription. "To the Glory of God. In Memoriam Private F Woods (of Orford) Ist V.B. (P.W.V.) South Lancashire Regiment, who volunteered for active ser vice in South Africa,and died of enteric fever at Howick on the 15th September, 1900, aged 26 years. This tablet is erected by his schoolfellows and friends." The dedication service on Sunday evening was attended by the deceased's late comrades in the Service Company uuder the command of Captain Anpleton, and there was a large congrega- tion,which included the Mayoress and Miss Roberts and Major Buckten. The service opened with the singing of the hymn " Soldiers of Christ arise," and after the evensong to the third colle,t, the special sentences of (Psalmthade xa dead ier and ere al eds.s o nT ha se nl In otlhl oe w e d servicethe fcoorl iteheet sbaufrtiearl V which the tablet was unveiled by the Rector (Rev Canon Willis). It was covered by the Union Jack, and as this was removed the tablet was revealed. Bugler lime, of the Active Service Company sounded the " Last Call "on the bugle. By this touching inci- dent many were visibly affected. The chhir then rendered the anthem, "I will lift up mine eyes," Mr .1 Cann presiding at the organ. The sermon was preached by the Rector, who took for his text the words For I know that my Redeemer iiveth, and that He shall stand at the latter day upon the earth ; and though after my skin worms destroy this body, yet in my flash shall I Sea God, whom I shall see for my st' and mine eyes shall behold, and not another:: though my reins be consumed within me." He observed that they had assembled to do honour to the memory of a young man whom, be believed was well known in that district and who bad served his Queen and country in the South African War. He dicil there in the dis- charge of his duty. Their action that night was to per- petuate his memory,and in doing so to give honour and glory to God and to point out to the coming generation bow it was possible to serve God and to serve one's country, and that when they did so "The memory of the just was blessed " and would live for many genera- tions to come. He was sure that what had been done that evening would be a great comfort and solace to the relatives and friends. A collection was taken for the memorial fund, and et the close of the service, the organist played the Dead March in "Saul," the con- gregation standing. Before the service commenced the Service Company, under the direction of Captain Appleton, paid a visit to Orford Hall, where they were received by Mrs Beamont, who met them in the porch and shook hands with each man. A short inspection of the gardens was made. Private Woods was born at Orford, and was educated there. The idea of perpetuating his memory was first suggested by Mr T 8 Steel (himself an old scholar) who wrote to the schoolmaster. That gentleman consulted the curate (Rev G Vale-Owen) who readily fell in with the suggestion. A meeting of old scholars was called, and this having met with their aproval a representative committee was formed with the Rev Vale-Owen as chairman, Mr Cana as hon. secretary, sad Mr Steel as hon. treasurer. Appeals for subscriptions were issued, and these resulted in over £26 being collected. The work was entrusted to Messrs Williams and Clay. The tablet, which has the words which appear above on it, is of white marble, with a polished dark granite back ground. Besides the invitation given to Captain Apple- ton, several of the St. Helens men expressed their wish to join in the ceremony, and over fifty were present. On the suggestion of the Rev. W. Pollock-Hill (vicar of Padgett), who was unavoidably absent, it has been decided that the interest on any balance which mat' be left shall provide a special prize, entitled the "Fred Woods Memorial prize," to be each year presented to the most Successful scholar in the day schoole.</p>
        <p>TRUSS.—New washable waterproof is the most suitand for tropkcil climates ; it is light, clean, cool, comfortable. a.l, durable ; can be worn whilst bathing. WHITS &amp; WEIGHte Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw-street, opposi top of Bold-street, Liverpool, Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS. On Sunday afternoon a choir of 80 voices, se- lected from the valious churches in the di,triot, gave an excellent rendering ~f Mendel-s,lhn's "Hymn of Praise." The choir was assisted by Miss Hilton, soprano soloist,and Mr Norman Dawdswell, tenor, both of Wigan. All the choruses and solos were given in a finished style, under te conducto,- ship of Mr A. Knight. Mr W S Melling presided at the organ. There was a large congiegation, and the cpllebtion which was in aid of the choir funds amounted to £l4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>POLICE CASES</head>
        <p>POLICE CASES</p>
        <p>At the Flarlestown Police Station on Monday morning before Ntr J V Emmett-, Joseph Bissett (Earlestown), Thomas Golding (llaydock), NI ichn el Loftus (Newion),Thomas Kirkman (Newton) James Duffield, no fixed abode, Thomas Kelly (Flaydock) W 'Knowles (Newton) and Bernard Tierney were all charged with bing drunk and disorderley in Eariestown on Saturday night and were each fined 2s:Gd and costs.—James Brown was charged with beingdrunk and disorderly and with asaaulting P.C. Clarke by kicking him on the leg, and was remanded to Newton Petty Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>TRH BURNING FATALITY AT HARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>TRH BURNING FATALITY AT HARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Mr Brighou.e, coroner, held an inqueit at the Victoria Inn, Wargrave, on Friday morning, on the body of the child Shaw, who died from injuries re- ceived by burning under circumstances detailed last week. Mrs ghaw, the mother, stated in evi- dence that the child was just recovering from sick- ness and she had left it in the T 03131 for a few minute.. She heard the child scream and ran to it, but it was ail ablaze. Drs Valentine and Latham, who were passing were called in, but the child died ahout an hour afterwards. A verdict of "Acciden- tal deoto" cira. return-id, the jury at the same time pas lug a vote of condolence with the mother. Tile coron-r remarked that h 3usehol I ditties must be attended to.</p>
        <p>PAINTERS' PICNIC FROM EARLESTOWN. On S iturday the painters employed at the Viaduct Works, Earlestown, had their annual Picnic en Billinge. Leaving the Griffin Hotel at 1 o'clock in three wagonettes they reached the Holt Hotel at Billings near the Causeway, shortly before three, the drive through the country being most enjoyable the weather adding greatly t ) the plea- sure. A field a short distance from the hotel was placed at the disoosal of the party which numbered 73, and here a 100 yards handicap was run off, E Ilicott, proving the winner. A capital knife and fork tea was partaken of and thoroughly enjoyed. A bowling handicap was an item in the dav's outing and ttilis proved more amusing than xdting. Some time was spent in harmony and Billings was left behind at 10 o'clock, Carlestown being reached about midnight. The arrangements were in the hands of Hr G Bunting and they proved most succe€sful.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL, EAHLHS- TOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual treat to the scho'ars attending the above school and the Mission school took place on Saturday afternoon. The scholars to the number of 1,300 assembled at the school, and beaded by the Liverooll Farm School Band, and accompanied by the Wsrrington Borough Band, marched in prom).- sloe through the streets of the town, to the church. where a short service was held by the Rev T R Mathews. After the service a pro- cession was again formed, and proceeded to the grand stand at the Race Course,where the scholars amused themselves in the ground adjoining until 'ea time. Tea was .erved in the grand stand.the wants of those present being attended to by the teachers and Nurse Irwin's Bible Class The scholars were after- wards amused by racing and skipping until dusk, the band playing for dancing, which was indulged in by the elders. The day was beautifully flne,and the children enjoyed the romp on the grass.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EARLESTOWN BAPTIST BACHELORS.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN BAPTIST BACHELORS.</p>
        <p>A party of bachelors connected with the Earl- town Baptist Sunday School to the number of about 30. had a picnic on Saturday to Chester. They left Earles own at 8 47, a saloon being provided for their anPororandation, and they reached Chester about 10 o'clock. They then proceeded to vi sit the Cathedral and other items of interest in the City, and afterwards adjourned to Bolland's restaurant where a capital dinner was partaken of. The after noon was agreeably spent by a sail up the Dee and a vi-it to Eaton Hall. On retnrning to the City. the inner rain was again refreshed at the restau- rant. During the day singing was freely indulged In, the nazawattee Glee party taking part. Amongst the bachelors it might be mentioned was loft. E Beaven, who has just returns,' from South Africa with the Volunteer Section of the South I Lancashire Regiment. Chelter was left behind a little before 9 o'clock, Earlestown being reached at 10 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>NEWTON :11ACIIINISTS' PICNIC</head>
        <p>NEWTON :11ACIIINISTS' PICNIC</p>
        <p>The machinemen ef Messrs McCorquodale and Co. hPld their first annual picnic on Saturday. the destination being Southport. The party left New- ton at 11.10 and arrived in Southport about 12 15. After strolling about for an hour or so they ad- journed to the Hoghton Arms where an expellent dinner was partaken of and thorougly enjoyed. After dinner the majority of the party arranged a drive to Kew where they indulged in a game of bowls and " beer " After tea they had a convivial hour at the hotel where some excellent songs wPre rendered by Mr J Howard, Mr T Ratcliffe, Mr J Ellicott, Mr S O'Kell, Mr 0 Neill and others. Mr J Aspinall accompanied on the piano,and Mr Mather, one of the invited friends, came to the rescue with a good recitation or two. They then made, the;r way home which the reached about 11 0 p.m , after spending one of the best days on the printers' record. The arrangements we're admirably carried out by Messrs F Brown and P Ball, to whom the thanks of the party are due.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>JOVIAL "COMPS."</head>
        <p>THE JOVIAL "COMPS."</p>
        <p>On Saturday last. the 6th inst the " Summer Boarders" of Wessre M'Corquodale &amp; Co., Newtor, journeyed to Lvmm. Prior to starting, the party were photographed on the Bowling Green of the Blue Bell. and departed en route at 1 45. Jovia iy and good fellowship was the order of the day, and right well dil the sons of • Caxton enter into the spirit, each seeking his own Pleasure in his fellows comfort. They formed an excellent group as they drove through the village, each deorated with a button-hole at the hands of Miss Waywell. The first stop wr44 made at the " Horse Shoe," W arrington, and advan- tage was taken by the " cotnps " to drink success to Mr Hibbert who had so kindly assisted arid con- tributed to the wayzgoose. The next stop being the "City Arms," Thelwall, and from there the party drove to the "Nag's Head" During the afternoon a series of sports was in- dulged in, Mr Harding winning the bowl- ing handicap, Mr Young the hundred yarde race, and in the "putting the weight" contest. "The Orphan" beat Mr Kendrick in the final by two inches, after some splendid throwing After seeing the sights of Lvmm toe party journeyed back to the" Blue Bell" where the rest of the even- ing was spent in harmony. The health of the firm was well received, and a warm tribute was paid to " The Orphan," who had the arrangements in hand. for the success of the venture. A most enjoyable day was brought to a close by that time-honoured finale "Auld Lang Syne."</p>
        <p>FOUND DROWNED AT EARLESTOWN. • On Sunday evening about half-past eight as collier named Robert flolcroft, living at Newton Common, was walking by the side of the Sankey Canal between the Common Bridge and the Ship Inn, he noticed the body of a man floating on the water near the bank. He got hold of the jacket and pulled the body out, and then sent to inform the 13o1ice, who removed it on the ambulance litter to the mortuary. The deceased man appeared to be about:4o years of age,and was fully dressed with the exception that his head was uncovered, The body was very much decomposed, and had apparently been in the water several days, the face being almost unrecognisable.—An inquest on the body was held at the Victoria Inn. Wargrave, on Wednes- day morning by Mr F A Jones, deputy coroner.— Constable Leimie said he removed the body to the mortuary. On searching it he found five florins, one half-crown, one sixpence, one penny, three halfpennies and a farthing rolled up in a white handkerchief in the lining of the right hand trouser pocket. Tbere were no initials on the handkerchief and nothing else on deceased. He had evidently been in the water two or three weeks, as the body was very much decomposed. The deceased was about 40 years of age and dressed in a dark grey jacket, brown tweed vest, dark worsted trousers, grey woollen singlet, black and grey striped flannelette shirr, grey socks and a pair of heavy blucher boors with hobnails iron heels and tips. He had a woollen searf tied round his neck in q peculiar fashion. The jury returned a verdict of ' 'Fond drowned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>CONGREGATIONAL SUNDAY ECHOOL.</head>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL SUNDAY ECHOOL.</p>
        <p>In connection with this school a picnic took place on Saturday to Frodsham. About 70 of tne scholars were conveyed in two capacious wag:miler tee pro- vi led by Massrs Worslay of 143wton. The day was gloriously fine and leaving Newton at one o'clock a couple of hours drive through the charming coun- try brought them to Frolshatn. Rambles among the hills, occupied the tima until tea, which was served at Hersey View, and heartily enjoyed. Foe remainder of the time was vary pleasantly spent, and leaving Frodshana about 8 o'clock, the party reacher! Newton about 10, after a thoroughly enjoy- able outing,the arrangements,wpioh had been made by 'llessrs Brimmer and Mc lardy being perfect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN• [BY A CORRESPOND':NT.]</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN• [BY A CORRESPOND':NT.]</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>For once in a way on Baturday Earlestown secur,d a victory, the vanquished on this occasion being Manchester Cliff rd. It cannot be said that the ,oancheater team is a very good one, and accordingly Ealltstown had an easy thing on. Earlestown's Sc tre was 108, out of which Rylance made 32, and Burrell 21, by very gooi cricket. Miller the profes:ional who is not playing in any- thing like form,was bowltd the first ball he received and Husband did very little better. In the attack Hatton, a promising player, did excellently with the ball taking sof he CliffJrd wickets.</p>
        <p>The second team went to Manchester, and although it was chiefly composed of the junior members of the club, still it was capable enough to get the upper hand of Alarichestor Clifford second eleven. The score was Flarlestown 99, out of which Butler made 25 and Rues 22, and Manchester Clifford 72.</p>
        <p>Newton-le-Willows had a day off on Saturday, so far as the first eleven was c•Jucerned, owing to the tie for the McGorquo..ale Challeoge Cap being played on that day at Leeds. To competition is between the Newton, Leeds, and London establishments of Messrs McCo quodale and Company, and as the makaity of the players in the Newton-le-Willows team are employees of the firm, they, of course,took part in the tie at Leeds. The Newton works are the holders of the cup, having for some time been invincible, lout this year they ware a woebegone expression as they returned to Newton, having re- ceived such a maulingat the hands of Leeds, as to put them to wondering how such a thing came about. Leeds piled on 160, but Newton with six wickets down had rtt scored a single rap. At the finish they only totalled 44, and out of these C W Elindley made 18, and &amp;shell eleven. The study- ing cap is now on, and evidently Newton will have to keep it on another 12 months.</p>
        <p>Newton-le-Willows second eleven had Glazebury as visitors on Saturlay, but whilst Glazabury only compiled 31, Newton put together 183 tor the loss of only 8 wickets. Rylance played a free game for 52, and Herring a new member of the team put on 36 In bowling Rylance did a fine piece of work by taking 7 wickets for 8 runs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>MR. G. L. JESSOP ON FIELDING.</head>
        <p>MR. G. L. JESSOP ON FIELDING.</p>
        <p>" In fielding in the slips the most desirable attitude is a slight stoop fora ard, with the hands held forward ready for I catch. The fielder must be ever on the alert, must riot snatch at the ball, and should be careful to always stand in the place he is put, as an alteration of even a foot often means the aifference between a catch missed and a catch held Short-slip should always back the wicket-keeper and be ready to run after ' snicks' and byes. Point shoulu not be regarded as a place where one may indulge in afternoon naps with impunity, although it is fuquently looked upon as a place where to ' bide' toe • rsboits ' of the aide. It is a difficult place to field in, and requires quickness and sureneas r,f eye. It is utterly useless to stand too far away, a is the common mistake; on a slow wicket one shouid come in quite close ; as the ball is apt to get on to he shoulder of the bat and go uo in the air, he la often the only man able to get to it. When fielding at • thi,d man ' one should be like a sprinter—always on his toes, as it is not easy to save short runs ; the slightest fumbling and a stolen run is the consequence. The ball comes off more awkwardly than at most places, as there it has generally some bias. One is those likely to run a man out by returning to the bowler than to the wicket-keeper when short runs are attempted, as the striker does not get into his stride as quickly as the non-striker. There is no place that requires more all-round lidding than • cover ' ; the posi- tion is somewhat similar to 'third man, though cover' has many more duties. He has to be continually on the alert in order to prevent short runs, must also back ' point,' and be ever ready to sprint for the hits that escape the vigilance of • point.' Everything hit to him, whether catches or ground balls, have screw 'en them catches resulting from mis-hits, that to the ordinary on. looker seem so ridiculously simple, are, indeed, amongst the hardest that one could have, as, in addition to the • bias ' resulting from such a hit, or rather mis-hit, the ball very often curls in the air and makes the catch donbly hard to judge. ' Cover ' should not stand to deep, especially when.' slow bowler is on, thou:. h h. position:ovaries accordiusto the wicket ; when there la no 'r xtra cover 'he has 71a immense amount of groune to look after, and should take care not to be too clos to • point.' In returning the ball he should do so from below the shoulder. One of the easiest positions to field i in is ' mid-off,' as n most cases the ball is generally hit straight at you; it is a place that r( quires a consider - able amount of pluck, as the ball comes very fast. • Mid-, if' must always back up the bowler, especially when there is a hard return, as it is extremely difficult for the bowler, when on the move, to stop the ball ; but as he often partially does so. by backing up a run may often be saved. He should not be in too close— except on sticky wickets—as he will not be in a position to back up properly. He should also be able to throw with preoision,as he often has an opportunity of running a man out. ' Mid-on ' might be termed ' rabbit-hutch,' for it is here that the • rabbits ' of a side are generally ' hidden,' as with ordinary fortune, they are here less likely to have a catch than in any other position. A good field in this position can often save his side many short rune. 'Square-leg' is another place in which it is custornary,to put incompetent fielders,though it is really a difficult position to fill. The ball comes very quick, and with a considerable amount of 'twist' on ; one is often unable to tell when a batsman is going to play.one in that direction. Bowlers who are confident of their ability not to bowl on the leg side rarely have a man at short or square leg when the wicket is good ; but when the ground is on the soft side, it is very necessary to have a man there. 'Long leg,' except to 'lob' and leg- brake bowlers, is a thing of the past ; but when used he should be a fast runner and a good ground field. Catches in this position are few and far between ; but when one does come they are generally diffictilt,the ball curling considerably to the right of the fielder."— Fran the July number cf the "Windsor Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>DEAI H FROM THE HEAT. A Sad Case at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>DEAI H FROM THE HEAT. A Sad Case at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court, on Wednesday evening. Mr R. Dobson, coroner, held an inquest on the body of William Hancock, a labourer, of Lock-street, Runcorn, who was found dead in a pert of the Old Soapery Works, on the previous evening, by'a fellow labourer. Thomas Hancock. 47 Princess-street, Runcorn, fore- man at Hazlehurst's Soap Works,ideritified the body as that of William Hancock, his brother. Deceased resided at 33 Lock-street, and was employed as a scrap- boiler. Ile was 56 years of age. Within the last 12 months deceased had not enjoyed very good health, and suffered from a bad cough. Witness would not describe him as a strong man; It would be about five minutes past five when he last saw his brother, on which occasion he gave him some instruction about his work. Deceased had suffered from influenza about twelve months previous, and remained in feeble health. He complained of shortness of breath and faintness. It was very hot where the deceased worked in consequence of these Am from a pan which contained about 25 tons of scar-. The room would be about 30 yards by 20 yards land contained seven pans. Only two of the pans were boiling,however. The room was pretty well ventilated. At the time of deceased's death there were only two persons working in the room. It would be about 25 minutes past five when his attention was called to his brother. George Sefton, 41, Nelson-strert, labourer, testified to being employed at the Soap Works. He was sent on a message by Mr Pope to Thomss Hancock, the foreman, and in passing the pan house be saw the deceased lying full length, face downwards on seine planks between the blue mottle house and the pan house. He shook deceased, but getting no answer, called for help. When help came deceased was carried into the air, and a doctor was sent for.</p>
        <p>_ Dr McDougall said he was called to the deceased at about half past five by a messenger. Deceased was in a sitting posture on a soap box ; and two or three men were trying to hold him up. He found the man to be dead, and that it would be useless to attempt artificial respiration. lie came to the conclusion that heart failure was the cause of death. This was probably superinduced by a weak heart,the excessive heat of the previous day; and the heat of the pan house. He could not help saying it was a pity there was no ambulance man present who could have commenced artificial re- spiration_in time.</p>
        <p>, A verdict in accordance with the medical testimony was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>[TALTON LADIES' CM:IL—OLIO Of the few F.3male Friendly Societies, having a distinctness and history' entirely its own, is the " Halton Ladies' Club," and 03 Monday last Halton 'village was sa fete in celebration of the 85th anniversary of this interesting club. Lads Brooke, of Norton Priory, the patroness of the sociert in accord with the usual practice headed a procession or members which made a circuit of the village, and ultl. mately reached the Parish Church, where a special service was conducted by the Rev E S Oliver, Runcorn. —A members meeting was held subs; quently, presided over by Lady Brooke. The general statement of amnia° I showed the membership at about 238, with a subscription of £l5l 9s. There had been paid in sickness fees £B3 83; funerals, £2O ; and services of sur.ean, £36 16s. The total worth of the society was £1,615 Os ld.—Tea served in a spacious marquee erected on the famo3s bowling greer, within the castle grounds, and waS catered for by Mr J Walker, the proprietor of the Cagle Hotel. The evening concluded with dancing on the green to the strains of the Runcorn Volunl"Per Band.! With the exception of Messrs John H Gamon ano Thomas Wright, trustees ; Mr P R Wright, secretall I and Mr C Leach, auditor, the affairs of the club Ire entirely managed by females.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. a. N. WILLIAMSON.</p>
        <p>:niiIITCH OF THE STORY, MRS. WILLIAMSON'S ideal of a story for serial publication is that it should hive in it something of excitement, of suspense, of mystery, and even of horror,and these Characteristics will certainly be found in "A Oirl of the People." The heroine of the story, Sheila Cope, finds herself suddenly by the singular occurrences on the occasion of a visit to a theatre with her supposed mother Lady Cope. plunged in the midst of a series of mysterious happenings. The mystery concerns herself, her mother, and a woman she had seen In the stalls, and It Is one which is not solved for many a day, for Lady Cope, before any explanation c.n be given. dies suddenly of the shock and horror of the thing. Sheila then finds she was the adopted daughter of Lady Cope, and that her mother is a poor woman living in a squalid street in Peckam. Sir Roger Cope, a nephew of the dead lady, announces that no will was left, and that he is Lady Cope's heir.but he offers to marry Sheila, who, however, feels an in- vincible aversion to him, and goes hurriedly to her mother's house. Even here, she is not free from the Persecutions of Sir Roger Cope and his agehts. All her attempts to get work are thwarlcd, her money. and her jewellery, on which she had hoped to raise enough to live for some time, are stolen, and in her desperate plight there seems to be no resource for her but a leap from Waterloo Bridge into the darkly flowing Thames. A good blend comes to the rescue just in time in one John Bourke, a Labour M.P.,who proves the best of good Samaritan s,and between him and his protegee a very tender feeling of regard and affection springs up. Even from this haven. however, Sheila Is forced to liy, and matilt are the adventures she has to pass through, till the mystery of Lady Cope's death is cleared up, and a way made for Inr happiness and that of Bourke. It is a story not only of great sensational interest, but one in which passages of rare charm abound.</p>
        <p>The LITERARY WORLD recently said : " Everyone who makes a point of keeping level with the wolks of writers counting it their peculiar glory to make the &amp;eh creep knows that Mr.:. C. N. Williamson is among filght of sensational novelists. Mrs. Williamson shines as a manufacturer of eisbarate pots and as a provider of thrills." the first</p>
        <p>"A GIRL OF TH E PsoPLE," BY Mw. C. N. WrLtismioN,</p>
